WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.293 --> 00:00:04.470
- Good morning, everybody,

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:08.040
and welcome to the Board of NHS England.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.390
My name is Penny Dash,

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:11.553
and I am the Chair of NHS England.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:14.070
First of all, like to apologise

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:16.290
for starting this meeting 30 minutes late.

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:18.420
Apologies to those people who are here,

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:19.800
and for those people who are here,

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:21.270
thank you very much for joining us.

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:22.770
You are always welcome,

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:25.920
and to anybody looking at this online.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.480
Unfortunately, we were slightly
encumbered by the trains,

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.970
so apologies for that.

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:34.950
We will make up a bit of time,

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:35.970
but we've got enough time

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:38.040
to have a full and proper discussion

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.530
on everything that we need to.

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:42.540
We do have some apologies

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:46.740
from Louise Ansari and Jeremy
Townsend, two of our NEDs,

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:48.300
and from Meghana Pandit.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.370
And Aidan, welcome to you for joining.

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.013
And Claire Fuller is
also unable to be here.

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:57.210
In terms of declaration of interest,

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:58.560
those are all published.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:02.490
Does anyone have anything that
has not been published? No?

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:05.160
Okay, so, before we get into the content,

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:07.110
just want to do
introductions for everybody,

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:08.880
so if everyone could go round the table

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.860
and say who they are
and what their role is,

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.924
and Jo, we'll start with you.

00:01:12.924 --> 00:01:15.957
- Jo Lenaghan, Interim
Director General of People.

00:01:15.957 --> 00:01:18.990
- Mark Cubbon, National
Director for Planned Care.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:20.615
- Amanda Doyle, National Director

00:01:20.615 --> 00:01:23.520
for Primary Care and Community Services.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.590
- Aidan Fowler, National
Director of Patient Safety

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:27.390
and Deputy Medical Director,

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:28.893
covering for Meghana Pandit.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.910
- Matthew Style, Director
General for System Development.

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:36.240
- Sam Everington, nonexecutive
director and a clinician.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:37.710
- Hi, everybody, Sarah-Jane Marsh,

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:40.080
National Director of UEC and Operations.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.720
- David Bennett, nonexecutive director.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.190
- Jules Hunt, Interim Director

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:46.440
of Technology, Digital, and Data.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:49.170
- Robert Lechler, nonexecutive director.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:52.440
- Elizabeth O'Mahony, Interim
Director General of Finance.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.630
- Jim Mackey, chief executive.

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:56.490
- Andrew Morris, Deputy Chair.

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:58.260
- [Duncan] Duncan Burton,
Chief Nursing Officer.

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:01.860
- Paul Corrigan, nonexecutive director.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.530
- Rob Checketts, strategic advisor

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:06.810
with responsibility for communications.

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:09.270
- David Probert, Deputy CEO, NHS England,

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:10.440
and Interim Director General,

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:11.993
Performance and Delivery.

00:02:11.993 --> 00:02:14.310
- [Ravi] Ravi Gurumurthy,
nonexecutive director.

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:15.840
- Mark Bailie, nonexecutive chair

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.390
of the Digital Data and
Technology Committee.

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:21.420
- Glen Burley, Financial Reset
and Accountability Director.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.970
- Tom Kibasi, Executive
Director of Strategy.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:26.670
- John Lester, director
of Corporate Governance.

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:28.230
- Right, thank you very much.

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:31.500
So, first item is the minutes
from the last Board meeting.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.533
Does anyone have any comments
or any questions on those?

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:39.960
No? Okay, so we'll take those
as accurate and approve those.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:41.880
So we're going to come on to updates

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:43.620
from different committees.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:46.500
Start with the Strategy
Committee and David.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:50.730
- Yes, so, we had our
first inaugural meeting

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:51.843
a few weeks ago.

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:54.390
Covered four topics:

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:57.120
first of all, agreeing
our terms of reference

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:02.070
under a remit to ensure the NHS deliver

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:03.990
against our strategic priorities

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:06.843
as set out in the 10 Year Health Plan;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.153
secondly, talking about
how we can establish

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.020
consistent methodology and approach

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:18.400
to all the different
work that needs to go on

00:03:19.353 --> 00:03:22.230
to achieve our strategic priorities;

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:24.540
thirdly, taking a look at all the work

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:26.610
that is currently going on;

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:30.150
and then finally, talking
about how we make sure

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:34.410
that we are covering all
the appropriate topics

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:35.553
in the right way.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:41.313
- Does anyone have any
comments or questions?

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:44.433
So, I think just one from me, David.

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:48.990
How much do you think you'll be able

00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:52.290
to cover sort of on an annual cycle?

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:55.110
And what do you think are
the key priority topic areas

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:57.690
that we should be getting going with?

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:00.750
- Well, the coverage...

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:04.650
We talked about having 15
priorities for the committee,

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:07.590
which sounds like rather a lot,

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:12.270
but I think those won't all
be going on at the same time,

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:13.950
so I think we're probably looking

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:18.240
at five or six topics

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.880
that we will want to
actually do deep dives into

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.653
and really focus on.

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:28.050
I think, in the current position,

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:29.580
where we really need to focus

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:31.920
is on thinking

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:35.280
about how we pull all of
the different initiatives

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:37.080
that are going on together.

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.090
And that's both an issue of narrative,

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:40.230
how do you tell a story

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:43.050
that explains how all this fits together

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:46.980
to achieve the objectives
of the 10 Year Health Plan,

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:49.860
but also, at a very quantitative level,

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:53.670
how, in numbers terms, in
money, in people, and so on,

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:54.540
does this all add up.

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:57.196
So I think that'll be a particular
focus in the short term.

00:04:57.196 --> 00:04:59.335
- Okay, great, thank you.

00:04:59.335 --> 00:05:01.410
Okay, so Andrew,

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:04.110
you're going to do Finance,
Performance, and People.

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:04.980
- Thank you.

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:08.493
So, we had our first Finance
and Performance meeting.

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:11.820
We look at every aspect of performance.

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:15.060
It's certainly a big kind
of a horizon of things,

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:18.600
and there's been a big
focus on financial delivery

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:19.890
for this year.

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:22.397
So you've got the notes
of the meeting there.

00:05:22.397 --> 00:05:25.050
I just want to pick out
three points, if I may.

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:28.500
The first one is around deficit support.

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:31.080
So, we've put in two billion this year,

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:34.743
mainly into providers and some systems.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:40.354
And a lot of time has been devoted,

00:05:40.354 --> 00:05:43.190
you know, if you look at
Elizabeth and Jim's time,

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:44.220
in trying to ensure

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:48.990
that boards deliver their
respective financial target,

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:51.090
because it's really, really
important that they do

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:53.730
and any slippage has a significant impact

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:55.323
on our overall numbers.

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:57.510
So we've agreed

00:05:57.510 --> 00:06:02.430
that the deficit support
regime should last

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:04.620
a maximum of three years,

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:08.370
this being the first of
those three years, or second.

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:09.360
Second?

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:10.207
- [Elizabeth] Year zero.

00:06:10.207 --> 00:06:12.093
- "Year zero," okay, sorry.

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:16.080
And we obviously want to
wean ourselves off it,

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:17.760
for clear reasons.

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:20.790
Second point is around capitation,

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:23.820
and the NHS has lots of debates over time

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:27.150
around how fast do we move to capitation,

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:31.080
but we feel we should make
some fairly swift progress,

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:36.060
recognising there are the
significant underfunders

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:38.727
in terms of capitation

00:06:38.727 --> 00:06:43.500
and that, in essence, we don't
want to destabilise the ones

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:45.000
that are at the other end of the scale.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:48.420
So we want to do that
over a kind of timely way.

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:52.380
And the third thing is
around system reform

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:54.660
in terms of payment systems.

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:58.500
So, we've approved the 20% marginal rate

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:02.280
for ED activity for next year

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:06.120
and using the tariff to
deliver bigger strategic shifts

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:08.190
in terms of shifts to community,

00:07:08.190 --> 00:07:12.060
outpatient reform in particular,

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:17.060
and also looking at, you
know, best practise tariffs

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:19.020
and how we can utilise those

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:22.740
to change the way we operate our services,

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:26.880
particularly in the shift
from acute into community.

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:28.293
So they're the kind of three big things

00:07:28.293 --> 00:07:30.333
that I think the meeting focused on.

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:34.563
- Great, questions? Observations?

00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:37.160
Okay, on to People.

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:42.090
- Okay, so, three things
to pick up, if I may.

00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:45.903
The first one is around a
voluntary redundancy scheme.

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:48.813
So, the scheme is now open,

00:07:50.077 --> 00:07:52.470
and it was signed off by the committee.

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:55.320
And as in previous mergers,

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:59.010
the People Committee has
agreed to take an overview.

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:03.690
We've got a further meeting
on the 17th of this month,

00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:06.960
mainly to ensure that the
arrangements that are in place

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:09.480
are fair and equitable and to ensure

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:14.250
that, basically, there's
no adverse equality impact.

00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:18.330
We did this last time round,
with the last restructuring,

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:21.813
and we feel we ought to focus,
as a committee, on this.

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:26.640
So, we've got our next
meeting on the 17th, as I say.

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:28.350
And then we'll just take stock

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:30.780
on how many people have come forward

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:32.760
and what the arrangements
are going forward

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:34.413
in terms of time-tabling.

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:40.020
Second point is around
recruitment of chairs and NEDs.

00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:43.860
So, there's a national appointments
team are pushing forward

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:45.780
a recruitment campaign.

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:47.970
We intend to try and recruit people

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:51.300
from all sectors, from all walks of life,

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:55.441
who have got lots of
experience to offer the NHS.

00:08:55.441 --> 00:08:59.523
And you know, we particularly
want to focus on diversity,

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:04.350
trying to appeal to a
younger cadre of people

00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:05.940
to join boards,

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:08.220
trying to appeal to people

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:11.400
from the private sector, in particular,

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:14.850
and also trying to urge employers

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:17.520
to encourage, you know,
some of their directors

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:21.360
to join the NHS as a nonexec.

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:23.403
And the same will apply to chairs.

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:29.070
You know, there's a shortage
in some parts of the country

00:09:29.070 --> 00:09:30.510
in terms of getting good NEDs,

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:31.830
and it's really, really important

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:34.650
that we do this work
very, very thoroughly.

00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:38.580
So the programme of recruitment rollout,

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:40.860
I think, will be key going forward,

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:42.150
and we'll, hopefully, get

00:09:42.150 --> 00:09:44.733
a really, really, you
know, fantastic response.

00:09:46.650 --> 00:09:49.743
The third thing is around culture.

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:51.750
So, we had a conversation

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:53.520
around the organisational development

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:55.247
of, you know, the new entity,

00:09:56.109 --> 00:09:58.590
DH and NHS England.

00:09:58.590 --> 00:10:01.650
And I think we do need to
spend a bit of time on culture.

00:10:01.650 --> 00:10:03.150
What is it that we want to see?

00:10:03.150 --> 00:10:07.350
What is it the staff want
to see going forward?

00:10:07.350 --> 00:10:08.880
How can we create something

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:11.610
that's very different and very new?

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:14.760
We said we would do this
when we had the last merger.

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:18.000
And two years on, we're
having to focus on this again.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.100
But I think it's really, really important,

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:22.830
and meanwhile, we need
to do business as usual

00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:27.660
in terms of appraisals and, you
know, being a good employer.

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:29.160
So I think that's a big focus

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:31.860
for the People Committee going forward.

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:34.560
And incidentally, Louise should
be doing this presentation.

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:36.325
I'm just her mouthpiece-

00:10:36.325 --> 00:10:38.190
- [Penny] Stand-in.

00:10:38.190 --> 00:10:39.023
- for today.

00:10:39.023 --> 00:10:39.856
Thank you.

00:10:39.856 --> 00:10:42.813
Oh, sorry, one final point
was on regional chairs.

00:10:44.130 --> 00:10:46.410
So, we're about to go out to ad-

00:10:46.410 --> 00:10:47.490
- [Penny] Have gone out to add.

00:10:47.490 --> 00:10:48.390
- on chairs.

00:10:48.390 --> 00:10:49.230
- [Penny] Yes, it's live.

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:54.230
- So they will support the seven
RDs in each of the regions,

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:57.540
network with all the chairs in the patch,

00:10:57.540 --> 00:11:00.210
be involved in chair appraisals,

00:11:00.210 --> 00:11:02.340
and have a line relationship to Penny.

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:06.880
And hopefully, they will
just add some capacity

00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:10.020
in that particular level of the system,

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:12.090
which I think is much-needed.

00:11:12.090 --> 00:11:13.080
Thank you.

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:15.333
- And quick question, Andrew.

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:20.730
Given that we are placing a lot,

00:11:20.730 --> 00:11:22.050
as we all come through everything,

00:11:22.050 --> 00:11:24.360
on our trust boards

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:25.890
and our wider governance systems,

00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:30.030
our ICB boards, and so on,

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:31.380
how do we make sure

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:33.150
that we really have the calibre

00:11:33.150 --> 00:11:34.500
of chairs and nonexecs

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:36.210
that we are looking for?

00:11:36.210 --> 00:11:37.650
Because you were talking about going broad

00:11:37.650 --> 00:11:39.987
and the risk is that,
then, we don't have people

00:11:39.987 --> 00:11:42.900
who can really interrogate
the data and the-

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:45.030
- So, I think it's

00:11:45.030 --> 00:11:47.610
about, you know, creating
a development programme

00:11:47.610 --> 00:11:49.560
when people are in post,

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:51.040
buddying with others

00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:55.503
going forward.

00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:01.060
It's trying to get people
with lots of experience

00:12:02.130 --> 00:12:07.130
of, you know, managing a board
or working at board level,

00:12:07.230 --> 00:12:09.903
and these things you learn on the job.

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:14.100
Historically, the NHS has
not been particularly good

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:15.720
at developing those skills,

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:17.820
and I think, going forward,

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:19.470
it's something that we should be doing,

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:22.530
and I know Jim is keen on this,

00:12:22.530 --> 00:12:25.413
around how do we develop our top leaders.

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:29.670
You know, a trust has a key
partnership, in my opinion.

00:12:29.670 --> 00:12:32.070
It's the chair, and it's the chief exec.

00:12:32.070 --> 00:12:34.380
When they work well together,

00:12:34.380 --> 00:12:38.280
the whole board tends to
work really, really well.

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:41.610
If you've got some dissonance,
then you've got tension.

00:12:41.610 --> 00:12:45.870
But you know, working
out that relationship,

00:12:45.870 --> 00:12:47.150
if you're a chief exec or a chair,

00:12:47.150 --> 00:12:48.750
is really, really important,

00:12:48.750 --> 00:12:50.850
and some people do it really, really well.

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:53.250
Some do it less well,
and we want to make sure

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:55.863
that everyone does it, you
know, fantastically well,

00:12:57.120 --> 00:12:59.190
for obvious reasons.

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:02.310
So I think, you know, we should be

00:13:02.310 --> 00:13:04.770
pushing harder at a development programme

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:07.656
for each of those groups of individuals.

00:13:07.656 --> 00:13:10.320
- Yeah, okay, so, yep, Sam.

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:11.940
- I suppose my question, Andrew, is

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:16.410
how do we get clinical
leadership engagement

00:13:16.410 --> 00:13:17.490
at that sort of level

00:13:17.490 --> 00:13:19.170
and have we got the balance right,

00:13:19.170 --> 00:13:21.660
are there enough clinicians on the board,

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:23.730
because the decisions they make every day

00:13:23.730 --> 00:13:25.980
often have very big impact

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:30.980
on finances, on the system,
and on population health.

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:33.663
- So I think, again,

00:13:35.490 --> 00:13:38.220
if you look internationally,
generally speaking,

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:41.130
there are more clinicians
in leadership positions

00:13:41.130 --> 00:13:43.927
than we have in the England NHS.

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:47.850
And I think the success of a good board

00:13:47.850 --> 00:13:51.460
is having a very, very
strong clinical voice

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:56.790
and, sitting below that, having
some sort of representation

00:13:56.790 --> 00:13:58.860
where people feel in a specialty

00:13:58.860 --> 00:14:00.220
that they have a voice

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:04.350
and they can express their opinion,

00:14:04.350 --> 00:14:08.133
have an input on key
decisions that a board makes.

00:14:09.869 --> 00:14:12.960
I think clinicians...

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:15.900
Over time, the relationships

00:14:15.900 --> 00:14:19.200
between the kind of board
level and the clinicians

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:23.640
has fractured a little in some
organisations, if I may say.

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:26.490
And you know, we need
to kind of win that back

00:14:26.490 --> 00:14:28.110
if we're to do some of the reforms

00:14:28.110 --> 00:14:30.480
that we intend to roll out

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:35.250
and make the service far more
kind of consumer-orientated.

00:14:35.250 --> 00:14:37.170
So I think it's a big challenge for us

00:14:37.170 --> 00:14:40.620
around getting the right
people in the right place

00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:43.140
and getting clinicians in, you
know, leadership positions.

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:47.310
There are relatively few
chief execs in England

00:14:47.310 --> 00:14:49.320
who have got a clinical background,

00:14:49.320 --> 00:14:52.530
and actually, you know, it should be more.

00:14:52.530 --> 00:14:54.840
It should be more because
being a chief exec

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:58.203
is a fantastic job, in my humble opinion.

00:14:59.130 --> 00:15:04.070
And you can, you know,
make significant change

00:15:04.070 --> 00:15:07.380
to the services and the
health of your local community

00:15:07.380 --> 00:15:09.090
And we should give clinicians

00:15:09.090 --> 00:15:11.700
a lot more opportunity to contribute.

00:15:11.700 --> 00:15:13.320
- [Penny] Robert?

00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:15.300
- Yeah, so in the terms of reference,

00:15:15.300 --> 00:15:17.160
you say the focus is going to be

00:15:17.160 --> 00:15:20.190
on the internal workforce of
the NHS, which makes sense,

00:15:20.190 --> 00:15:21.420
but my question is

00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:24.960
does that include the
10 Year Workforce Plan,

00:15:24.960 --> 00:15:29.040
which is a crucial delivery
part of the 10-year plan.

00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:31.080
And if it isn't addressed
in this committee,

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:32.811
where is it being sort of overseen?

00:15:32.811 --> 00:15:34.229
- [Penny] It's in the Strategy Committee.

00:15:34.229 --> 00:15:35.377
- Strategy Committee.

00:15:35.377 --> 00:15:39.171
- Yeah, yeah, and we've got
a time slot for it, as well.

00:15:39.171 --> 00:15:40.317
- Oh, right.
- Even better.

00:15:40.317 --> 00:15:42.753
- Our focus is predominantly NHS England.

00:15:44.280 --> 00:15:46.590
- Okay, so, thank you for that

00:15:46.590 --> 00:15:48.000
and for standing in for Louise.

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:48.840
- Thank you.
- And I think

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:51.090
we will come back in the new year

00:15:51.090 --> 00:15:53.460
to doing more about that
board-development work.

00:15:53.460 --> 00:15:56.093
I'm interested in that. So, Mark.

00:15:56.093 --> 00:15:57.090
- Digital Committee.

00:15:57.090 --> 00:15:59.730
So, one sort of general overview point

00:15:59.730 --> 00:16:01.440
and two specific ones.

00:16:01.440 --> 00:16:05.700
So, real conclusion from
Digital Maturity Assessment's

00:16:05.700 --> 00:16:09.330
that the more digitally
mature operations are

00:16:09.330 --> 00:16:10.780
the more productive they are.

00:16:12.150 --> 00:16:14.250
And we saw

00:16:14.250 --> 00:16:17.490
most of the papers that
then fell under that area

00:16:17.490 --> 00:16:22.230
were in the theme of how
do we take strategic intent

00:16:22.230 --> 00:16:24.630
and accelerate into action
and actual deployment

00:16:24.630 --> 00:16:27.240
so one discussion

00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:31.667
around how do we have very
clear robust processes

00:16:31.667 --> 00:16:33.120
around AI adoption

00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:35.970
that then allows us to
roll out ambient voice,

00:16:35.970 --> 00:16:39.243
which clearly has very
big frontline benefit;

00:16:40.080 --> 00:16:44.250
how we work much more closely
with the other directorates

00:16:44.250 --> 00:16:47.160
so neighbourhood, primary care, UEC

00:16:47.160 --> 00:16:49.383
about what triage really means.

00:16:52.109 --> 00:16:53.400
And that was a recurring theme

00:16:53.400 --> 00:16:55.020
in the importance of
modern service frameworks.

00:16:55.020 --> 00:16:57.040
Which, again, fully digitised, we think

00:16:57.040 --> 00:17:00.180
there are big productivity
benefits out there to be had.

00:17:00.180 --> 00:17:02.670
In terms of two specific discussions,

00:17:02.670 --> 00:17:06.210
we had a deep dive on the
Federated Data Platform.

00:17:06.210 --> 00:17:11.210
When it's deployed with six
or seven core components,

00:17:11.340 --> 00:17:13.410
it is having a clear benefit

00:17:13.410 --> 00:17:15.810
on interoperability within the trusts

00:17:15.810 --> 00:17:18.540
and the overall productivity
of those trusts.

00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:19.740
And I think the real question is

00:17:19.740 --> 00:17:22.950
how do we accelerate that
rollout and accelerate adoption.

00:17:22.950 --> 00:17:24.930
And then we had a deep dive

00:17:24.930 --> 00:17:28.500
into the first element of
the medium-term planning:

00:17:28.500 --> 00:17:30.600
digital triage in the app,

00:17:30.600 --> 00:17:31.650
what that really means,

00:17:31.650 --> 00:17:34.290
what actions and decisions have to be made

00:17:34.290 --> 00:17:36.720
for it to be practically implementable

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:38.310
as opposed to just an idea.

00:17:38.310 --> 00:17:41.850
So good progress. Still a lot to do.

00:17:41.850 --> 00:17:45.750
- Okay, and on that FDP times
six to seven components,

00:17:45.750 --> 00:17:49.320
how many trusts is that
active in now, roughly?

00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:50.640
Or you can ask a friend.

00:17:50.640 --> 00:17:52.443
- Ask a friend: Jules.

00:17:53.450 --> 00:17:57.810
- Okay, but I mean like 10%? 50%?

00:17:57.810 --> 00:17:58.950
- I'll have to check very quickly

00:17:58.950 --> 00:18:01.590
on the six that it's
deployed to, Chair, exactly.

00:18:01.590 --> 00:18:02.423
- [Mark Bailie] It is a...

00:18:02.423 --> 00:18:03.256
So, if you look at...

00:18:03.256 --> 00:18:07.650
The majority of deployments
do not have the full suite.

00:18:07.650 --> 00:18:10.953
Where the full suite is in
place, it is very effective.

00:18:11.940 --> 00:18:14.040
And there are often-used examples

00:18:14.040 --> 00:18:16.790
where we've seen it and it's
been embedded for a while.

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:19.290
- Okay. Elizabeth?

00:18:19.290 --> 00:18:20.820
- Yeah, thank you.

00:18:20.820 --> 00:18:22.350
We're doing quite a bit at the moment

00:18:22.350 --> 00:18:25.140
on the should-be costs,
as we're describing it,

00:18:25.140 --> 00:18:29.790
and how do we embed digital
in a number of the pathways.

00:18:29.790 --> 00:18:31.680
We're, obviously, doing
that in executive level.

00:18:31.680 --> 00:18:34.470
It'd be good to come to the
committee, maybe, and share

00:18:34.470 --> 00:18:36.360
and for you to test some
of what we're doing,

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:39.240
if that's an opportunity, Mark.

00:18:39.240 --> 00:18:40.440
- [Mark Bailie] Be great.

00:18:40.440 --> 00:18:41.910
- Please

00:18:41.910 --> 00:18:42.743
- Ravi?

00:18:42.743 --> 00:18:44.160
- [Ravi] On the electronic patient record,

00:18:44.160 --> 00:18:46.827
it says that 95% of trusts
have implemented it.

00:18:46.827 --> 00:18:48.240
Are we seeing that translate

00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:52.080
into clinical decision-making
and their behaviour?

00:18:52.080 --> 00:18:55.410
- I think this is a prime
example of the difference

00:18:55.410 --> 00:18:59.460
between strategy and deployment

00:18:59.460 --> 00:19:02.163
and operational experience.

00:19:04.830 --> 00:19:07.380
Anecdotally, and I've
asked for the data on it,

00:19:07.380 --> 00:19:09.090
some deployments of EPRs

00:19:09.090 --> 00:19:11.820
lead to a five-year dip in productivity,

00:19:11.820 --> 00:19:14.520
and it's because of embedding

00:19:14.520 --> 00:19:17.670
with actual clinical pathways

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:20.130
and training our colleagues

00:19:20.130 --> 00:19:21.750
to actually use the systems effectively.

00:19:21.750 --> 00:19:25.680
So when done well, they are
a definite force for good.

00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:29.013
The 95% does not reflect where done well.

00:19:31.950 --> 00:19:34.313
- I guess it was a reflection
on the comments around AI

00:19:34.313 --> 00:19:39.300
and the adoption of AI and clear guidance.

00:19:39.300 --> 00:19:40.133
It's just to reflect

00:19:40.133 --> 00:19:41.310
I think there's something really important

00:19:41.310 --> 00:19:44.700
about the professional regulators
keeping pace with that.

00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:45.533
And certainly, something

00:19:45.533 --> 00:19:46.830
that we have raised, or I've raised,

00:19:46.830 --> 00:19:49.140
with the Nursing and Midwifery Council

00:19:49.140 --> 00:19:52.045
is a review of the Code of
Conduct going on at the moment.

00:19:52.045 --> 00:19:53.250
I think it's an important part

00:19:53.250 --> 00:19:54.810
of making sure that these keep pace,

00:19:54.810 --> 00:19:56.070
and I'm sure Aidan's got reflections

00:19:56.070 --> 00:19:57.900
on medical side of things.

00:19:57.900 --> 00:19:59.790
- Yeah, I think there's...

00:19:59.790 --> 00:20:01.650
Well, as we've sort of neatly shown,

00:20:01.650 --> 00:20:05.670
there's clearly interfaces
between the clinical work,

00:20:05.670 --> 00:20:07.770
the care pathways, and the technology,

00:20:07.770 --> 00:20:09.330
and we've got to keep coming back

00:20:09.330 --> 00:20:11.063
to bringing all that together, yep.

00:20:12.570 --> 00:20:15.210
Okay, good.

00:20:15.210 --> 00:20:16.890
So, hand over to you, Jim?

00:20:16.890 --> 00:20:18.600
- Yep, thanks, Penny.

00:20:18.600 --> 00:20:21.750
So I'm going to just do a
bit of a general update first

00:20:21.750 --> 00:20:24.930
on things that've
happened since last Board

00:20:24.930 --> 00:20:26.490
but then spend a bit more time

00:20:26.490 --> 00:20:28.350
talking about where we
are with regard to winter,

00:20:28.350 --> 00:20:30.690
just to tee up Sarah-Jane a little bit,

00:20:30.690 --> 00:20:33.090
but also industrial action.

00:20:33.090 --> 00:20:34.830
So we'll start and end
with industrial action.

00:20:34.830 --> 00:20:37.770
First of all, a thanks for everybody here

00:20:37.770 --> 00:20:39.810
but especially out in the service

00:20:39.810 --> 00:20:42.330
for how the last round was managed,

00:20:42.330 --> 00:20:45.960
building on progress, or the
experience, in the summer.

00:20:45.960 --> 00:20:48.420
Really, really fantastically managed.

00:20:48.420 --> 00:20:50.160
I think it's extremely sad

00:20:50.160 --> 00:20:51.510
that we're talking about getting very good

00:20:51.510 --> 00:20:53.839
at something none of us want to do

00:20:53.839 --> 00:20:56.697
so a sort of sad reflection
of where we are now,

00:20:56.697 --> 00:20:58.740
but the service did an incredible job.

00:20:58.740 --> 00:21:00.780
And on management information, obviously,

00:21:00.780 --> 00:21:02.443
to go through all the QA,

00:21:02.443 --> 00:21:06.060
a very, very strong
month electively, oddly,

00:21:06.060 --> 00:21:07.710
in a month of industrial action.

00:21:07.710 --> 00:21:10.320
Mark will share that when the data's been

00:21:10.320 --> 00:21:14.010
through all the QA processes, et cetera.

00:21:14.010 --> 00:21:15.990
Just going back to general updates.

00:21:15.990 --> 00:21:17.370
So, there's an awful lot happened.

00:21:17.370 --> 00:21:19.560
Every time we have one
of these sessions...

00:21:19.560 --> 00:21:21.330
I think, in reality, we're on dog years,

00:21:21.330 --> 00:21:24.603
so three months feels
like two years or so.

00:21:25.440 --> 00:21:29.520
We've had medium-term
planning frameworks issued

00:21:29.520 --> 00:21:33.090
after a very long complicated
discussion with government

00:21:33.090 --> 00:21:36.120
about allocations and how
all that works, et cetera,

00:21:36.120 --> 00:21:40.050
again, allocations out for two years,

00:21:40.050 --> 00:21:41.940
real development on neighbourhoods,

00:21:41.940 --> 00:21:44.280
the advanced foundation trust pipeline,

00:21:44.280 --> 00:21:46.950
progress on IHOs, et cetera.

00:21:46.950 --> 00:21:47.817
When you stand back from that

00:21:47.817 --> 00:21:50.370
and you see what the
NHS' actually coping with

00:21:50.370 --> 00:21:53.070
as well as day-to-day
operational pressures.

00:21:53.070 --> 00:21:54.570
And I think real evidence

00:21:54.570 --> 00:21:56.760
that people are starting
to think of the future

00:21:56.760 --> 00:21:57.810
as well as today, as well,

00:21:57.810 --> 00:22:00.360
which is what that medium-term
planning things about,

00:22:00.360 --> 00:22:03.570
and I think it's absolutely
incredibly impressive.

00:22:03.570 --> 00:22:06.000
But as always, still a lot to do.

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:07.410
We've still got to land this year.

00:22:07.410 --> 00:22:09.270
We've got to get through winter.

00:22:09.270 --> 00:22:12.630
We've got to, you know, get
decent plans in for three years

00:22:12.630 --> 00:22:13.900
that deliver what we need to do.

00:22:13.900 --> 00:22:17.070
But I think we should
again acknowledge progress

00:22:17.070 --> 00:22:19.830
in how big a leap that
has been for the service.

00:22:19.830 --> 00:22:22.830
And also, as Elizabeth
will cover later on,

00:22:22.830 --> 00:22:25.425
being broadly on plan at
this point in the year

00:22:25.425 --> 00:22:27.420
is, you know, really impressive, again,

00:22:27.420 --> 00:22:30.420
yeah, compared to previous rounds,

00:22:30.420 --> 00:22:32.730
Andrew's already mentioned, as well,

00:22:32.730 --> 00:22:34.890
as part of that discussion
with government,

00:22:34.890 --> 00:22:36.330
we've got agreement to progress

00:22:36.330 --> 00:22:39.150
on the voluntary redundancy scheme.

00:22:39.150 --> 00:22:43.860
So that's now progressing
actively here and out in ICB,

00:22:43.860 --> 00:22:46.110
so very frustrating.

00:22:46.110 --> 00:22:47.850
We've all recorded before

00:22:47.850 --> 00:22:51.330
how unhappy and disappointed we'd been

00:22:51.330 --> 00:22:54.270
in the way that that had been
handled, because of the delay,

00:22:54.270 --> 00:22:56.430
but at least, now people
are able to progress.

00:22:56.430 --> 00:22:59.940
And that's a live conversation
here and out in the service,

00:22:59.940 --> 00:23:02.550
so it'd be good to get that concluded

00:23:02.550 --> 00:23:05.043
as quickly and as effectively as we can.

00:23:06.600 --> 00:23:08.220
It feels like winter though, doesn't it?

00:23:08.220 --> 00:23:11.790
So, we're looking out the
windows there, and it's dark.

00:23:11.790 --> 00:23:14.430
It was nearly freezing when
I left home this morning.

00:23:14.430 --> 00:23:16.020
It is proper winter,

00:23:16.020 --> 00:23:19.170
and we've had a really,
really big increase in flu

00:23:19.170 --> 00:23:21.150
over the last few weeks.

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:24.510
We're at that point where
there's lots of sources of data.

00:23:24.510 --> 00:23:27.120
It takes a long time for the
data to sort of feed through

00:23:27.120 --> 00:23:31.620
and become more valid, more
technically sound, et cetera.

00:23:31.620 --> 00:23:32.880
But if you just stand back and look

00:23:32.880 --> 00:23:35.850
from the way the data's moving day by day,

00:23:35.850 --> 00:23:39.150
we are currently at around
2,000 occupied beds.

00:23:39.150 --> 00:23:40.440
I would think it's reasonable

00:23:40.440 --> 00:23:44.340
to assume we'll be between
5,000 and 8,000 occupied beds

00:23:44.340 --> 00:23:45.900
around this time next week.

00:23:45.900 --> 00:23:48.880
That's a material impact on our capacity

00:23:50.010 --> 00:23:50.910
as we're recovering

00:23:50.910 --> 00:23:53.640
from the last round of industrial action

00:23:53.640 --> 00:23:58.640
and just about, sadly,
heading to another one.

00:23:58.650 --> 00:24:00.420
On Monday, we'd set up a call

00:24:00.420 --> 00:24:01.710
with chief exec colleagues

00:24:01.710 --> 00:24:04.920
to just talk through that rise in flu

00:24:04.920 --> 00:24:07.020
and the operational
pressures that were evident

00:24:07.020 --> 00:24:08.850
as a result of that,

00:24:08.850 --> 00:24:11.760
and just as we started the
call, I got the message to say

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:15.600
that industrial action was
going to be called again.

00:24:15.600 --> 00:24:17.040
And I think, you know, to be fair,

00:24:17.040 --> 00:24:21.210
there's a genuine and palpable feeling

00:24:21.210 --> 00:24:24.963
of anger, frustration, exasperation.

00:24:26.610 --> 00:24:27.872
Lots of discussion since.

00:24:27.872 --> 00:24:30.150
I was talking to a chief exec
colleague on the way down.

00:24:30.150 --> 00:24:32.973
Spoke with some consultant
colleagues a few days ago.

00:24:33.840 --> 00:24:35.550
I think we're all just really trying

00:24:35.550 --> 00:24:38.850
to get our heads around
something that feels cruel,

00:24:38.850 --> 00:24:43.173
feels calculated to cause
mayhem, create harm,

00:24:44.250 --> 00:24:45.810
at a time when the service is really

00:24:45.810 --> 00:24:47.700
pulling all the stops out

00:24:47.700 --> 00:24:50.550
to try and avoid that
and keep people safe,

00:24:50.550 --> 00:24:54.270
so I really do hope we can
find a way of avoiding this.

00:24:54.270 --> 00:24:58.170
We can't expect this round to
be similar to the last two,

00:24:58.170 --> 00:25:01.950
because it's now being called
at the height of winter

00:25:01.950 --> 00:25:04.680
when we've got a lot of flu
around and we are actively

00:25:04.680 --> 00:25:08.790
considering what our national
response to that should be.

00:25:08.790 --> 00:25:10.590
And also, with colleagues
out in the service,

00:25:10.590 --> 00:25:13.560
we'll talk to to them on Monday again,

00:25:13.560 --> 00:25:16.320
yeah, to work through
what's most helpful to them

00:25:16.320 --> 00:25:18.600
to keep patients safe.

00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:20.970
So you sit back from all of that,

00:25:20.970 --> 00:25:23.343
you know, absolutely unbelievable progress

00:25:23.343 --> 00:25:25.770
in a very, very hard year,

00:25:25.770 --> 00:25:28.203
yeah, progress on multiple fronts.

00:25:29.040 --> 00:25:30.720
Still a lot to do. It's always a...

00:25:30.720 --> 00:25:31.770
You know, it's always
difficult at this point of year

00:25:31.770 --> 00:25:34.020
as people start thinking about Christmas

00:25:34.020 --> 00:25:35.610
and time with our families.

00:25:35.610 --> 00:25:37.500
Sadly, a lot of people
won't have that this year.

00:25:37.500 --> 00:25:39.900
Because of industrial action,

00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:42.900
they'll be having to work to
keep this show on the road.

00:25:42.900 --> 00:25:46.590
And genuine signs of optimism as well

00:25:46.590 --> 00:25:49.740
on that progress and the
ability to plan ahead

00:25:49.740 --> 00:25:53.890
but, again, tarnished with the
call for industrial action.

00:25:53.890 --> 00:25:55.170
And I just want to...

00:25:55.170 --> 00:25:56.310
I was going to quote a load of stats,

00:25:56.310 --> 00:25:58.320
but I'm just going to just dwell on one

00:25:58.320 --> 00:25:59.550
if you can bear with me for a minute.

00:25:59.550 --> 00:26:03.900
So, one of our very large
organisations, UHB, in Birmingham

00:26:03.900 --> 00:26:07.189
has been really challenged in
lots of ways over the years.

00:26:07.189 --> 00:26:10.050
And you look at what they've
achieved electively this year.

00:26:10.050 --> 00:26:12.213
It's absolutely unbelievable,

00:26:13.304 --> 00:26:16.990
a nearly 15% improvement
in RTT performance

00:26:18.486 --> 00:26:19.757
in a year

00:26:19.757 --> 00:26:22.487
and a reduction of 45,000

00:26:22.487 --> 00:26:24.800
on 143,000 in list size

00:26:24.800 --> 00:26:26.400
in a similar period,

00:26:26.400 --> 00:26:29.010
so when people are saying,
"Oh this isn't possible,"

00:26:29.010 --> 00:26:30.840
it's possible, because it's happened.

00:26:30.840 --> 00:26:32.460
It is happening.

00:26:32.460 --> 00:26:34.050
Their neighbourhoods are happening.

00:26:34.050 --> 00:26:36.090
IHOs are developing, you know, et cetera.

00:26:36.090 --> 00:26:39.180
So when we're handling ourselves

00:26:39.180 --> 00:26:40.440
and managing our grumpiness

00:26:40.440 --> 00:26:42.402
through industrial action
and various other things,

00:26:42.402 --> 00:26:44.550
I think we need to go back
to those sorts of things,

00:26:44.550 --> 00:26:46.650
and yeah, that is the stuff that...

00:26:46.650 --> 00:26:47.700
That gives us hope.

00:26:47.700 --> 00:26:50.250
So, I'll stop there and
take any questions, Penny.

00:26:50.250 --> 00:26:55.020
- Thank you, Jim, and thank you
for leading us at this time.

00:26:55.020 --> 00:26:56.703
So questions for Jim?

00:26:59.490 --> 00:27:00.843
Take Sam, yep.

00:27:02.100 --> 00:27:03.720
- Yeah, and I'm always slightly nervous

00:27:03.720 --> 00:27:06.210
about mentioning general
practise, because I'm a GP,

00:27:06.210 --> 00:27:09.060
but actually, it's been a great success,

00:27:09.060 --> 00:27:10.440
I think, the total triage.

00:27:10.440 --> 00:27:14.250
Massive increased use in online contact,

00:27:14.250 --> 00:27:16.500
requests for consultations.

00:27:16.500 --> 00:27:18.870
There are some problems and stresses

00:27:18.870 --> 00:27:21.660
that we clearly need to deal with,

00:27:21.660 --> 00:27:23.520
but it is the one part of the NHS

00:27:23.520 --> 00:27:25.122
patient satisfaction's going up.

00:27:25.122 --> 00:27:28.710
Productivity's up. You know,
it's a really good-news story.

00:27:28.710 --> 00:27:30.060
But also, I think we need

00:27:30.060 --> 00:27:31.780
to acknowledge all the hard work

00:27:32.640 --> 00:27:34.650
people are doing in primary care

00:27:34.650 --> 00:27:36.870
and recognise some of the stresses,

00:27:36.870 --> 00:27:38.640
like issues around continuity of care,

00:27:38.640 --> 00:27:39.473
that need to be addressed,

00:27:39.473 --> 00:27:41.580
so it'd be great to get
your thoughts on that.

00:27:41.580 --> 00:27:43.290
And then the much more wider issue;

00:27:43.290 --> 00:27:45.360
we sort of touched on it
with Andrew with the Board;

00:27:45.360 --> 00:27:47.430
is how we engage

00:27:47.430 --> 00:27:50.400
all the clinicians in the
circumstances, actually,

00:27:50.400 --> 00:27:52.230
of some of these industrial actions

00:27:52.230 --> 00:27:54.690
in transforming the NHS.

00:27:54.690 --> 00:27:56.280
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:27:56.280 --> 00:27:58.917
So I couldn't agree more on
the primary care thing, Sam.

00:27:58.917 --> 00:28:00.030
And I mean, if we're honest,

00:28:00.030 --> 00:28:02.580
when we look at those experience stats,

00:28:02.580 --> 00:28:03.750
I'm still a bit conditioned

00:28:03.750 --> 00:28:05.910
to look at them and expect bad news.

00:28:05.910 --> 00:28:07.860
The last few have been
really fantastic news,

00:28:07.860 --> 00:28:08.790
haven't they, Amanda?

00:28:08.790 --> 00:28:13.200
Just really powerful,
demonstrable, robust data

00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:15.630
that shows that people are actually happy

00:28:15.630 --> 00:28:17.370
with what's being done.

00:28:17.370 --> 00:28:19.500
But that is at the expense
of a lot of hard work

00:28:19.500 --> 00:28:20.880
of colleagues in primary care.

00:28:20.880 --> 00:28:22.770
Primary care's also really
stepped up, I think,

00:28:22.770 --> 00:28:24.150
on the neighbourhood conversation,

00:28:24.150 --> 00:28:26.280
and they are actively involved

00:28:26.280 --> 00:28:28.770
and driving those discussions.

00:28:28.770 --> 00:28:30.330
You know, whether people
have noticed or not,

00:28:30.330 --> 00:28:32.940
our conversations have shifted
more into that territory

00:28:32.940 --> 00:28:35.970
and slightly away from the
normal kind of hospital stuff,

00:28:35.970 --> 00:28:38.190
so I think real acknowledgement.

00:28:38.190 --> 00:28:39.510
But all of these things,

00:28:39.510 --> 00:28:41.390
and Glen and I talk about
this a bit, when we're talking

00:28:41.390 --> 00:28:42.806
about neighbourhoods,

00:28:42.806 --> 00:28:46.514
IHOs, the shift to community generally,

00:28:47.901 --> 00:28:50.370
it is reliant on leadership capacity

00:28:50.370 --> 00:28:55.370
but not traditional
leadership in every regard,

00:28:55.650 --> 00:28:57.240
especially the clinical leadership,

00:28:57.240 --> 00:29:00.150
including our nursing
colleagues and AHPs and others.

00:29:00.150 --> 00:29:02.940
And we've started a conversation this week

00:29:02.940 --> 00:29:04.590
about how we start.

00:29:04.590 --> 00:29:05.820
And we talked last time

00:29:05.820 --> 00:29:07.440
about leadership and management standards,

00:29:07.440 --> 00:29:09.390
about how we broaden that out

00:29:09.390 --> 00:29:11.460
to cover leadership in the round,

00:29:11.460 --> 00:29:14.190
so we can have a
conversation in the new year

00:29:14.190 --> 00:29:16.440
that addresses all of the
issues you've described

00:29:16.440 --> 00:29:19.427
but also make sure in our troubled places

00:29:19.427 --> 00:29:22.350
we can actually recruit chief executives,

00:29:22.350 --> 00:29:25.410
finance directors, nurse
directors, you know, et cetera,

00:29:25.410 --> 00:29:28.844
and start building a pipeline
for the future, as well.

00:29:28.844 --> 00:29:31.860
- Yep, absolutely. Andrew?

00:29:31.860 --> 00:29:35.460
- So, Jim, I think we've
seen a lot of boards

00:29:35.460 --> 00:29:39.390
step up to the plate this
year and really deliver,

00:29:39.390 --> 00:29:42.577
but I know a lot of boards are
still trying to square off:

00:29:42.577 --> 00:29:46.860
"Do I deliver the money
or some of the targets?

00:29:46.860 --> 00:29:48.660
And if I can't deliver the money,

00:29:48.660 --> 00:29:50.250
you know, what happens to the targets?"

00:29:50.250 --> 00:29:54.790
And I don't know what advice
you give these entities

00:29:55.710 --> 00:29:57.480
when they're faced with that situation

00:29:57.480 --> 00:30:00.660
about how do you trade
off some of these things.

00:30:00.660 --> 00:30:01.800
I mean, you've talked about UHB,

00:30:01.800 --> 00:30:05.610
and that performance on
electives has been fantastic,

00:30:05.610 --> 00:30:08.220
but you know, the
organization's quite challenged

00:30:08.220 --> 00:30:10.800
on the ED front on four hours,

00:30:10.800 --> 00:30:12.600
so it's just squaring it off.

00:30:12.600 --> 00:30:14.310
And I think a lot of boards

00:30:14.310 --> 00:30:17.160
are trying to make the
right call this year,

00:30:17.160 --> 00:30:19.620
and it's quite a difficult challenge

00:30:19.620 --> 00:30:22.470
if you're on the edge financially

00:30:22.470 --> 00:30:24.630
and you know you've
got a difficult winter.

00:30:24.630 --> 00:30:27.810
And ideally, you know, you've
got a bit more capacity

00:30:27.810 --> 00:30:31.160
that you could open, but you
haven't got the money to do so.

00:30:31.160 --> 00:30:34.050
And it's, you know, sorting
out those trade-offs

00:30:34.050 --> 00:30:36.030
that I think's really, really important,

00:30:36.030 --> 00:30:37.920
and making the right call,

00:30:37.920 --> 00:30:39.930
because what we don't want to see

00:30:39.930 --> 00:30:43.503
is boards making the wrong
call and failing on everything.

00:30:44.550 --> 00:30:46.320
That would be disastrous.

00:30:46.320 --> 00:30:47.153
- So I'll come back.

00:30:47.153 --> 00:30:48.870
Elizabeth and other
colleagues chip in as well.

00:30:48.870 --> 00:30:50.520
Really, really pertinent, Andrew.

00:30:50.520 --> 00:30:52.554
And first thing I would say

00:30:52.554 --> 00:30:54.420
is there's a risk in all this

00:30:54.420 --> 00:30:56.970
in an era when we're trying to devolve

00:30:56.970 --> 00:30:58.980
that we take the keys back off people.

00:30:58.980 --> 00:31:00.630
So, when they're juggling
with these things,

00:31:00.630 --> 00:31:03.000
we get too far into their decision-making.

00:31:03.000 --> 00:31:05.393
We need to help build boards

00:31:05.393 --> 00:31:07.410
so they have these conversations locally

00:31:07.410 --> 00:31:08.613
and make those choices.

00:31:09.570 --> 00:31:13.260
Myself and other colleagues
are regularly in conversation

00:31:13.260 --> 00:31:15.870
with people out there
who are juggling this

00:31:15.870 --> 00:31:17.700
and trying to give advice in the best way,

00:31:17.700 --> 00:31:19.320
provide support in the best way,

00:31:19.320 --> 00:31:21.840
because I think it is important
that the boards locally,

00:31:21.840 --> 00:31:24.150
with clinical colleagues,
have the conversation

00:31:24.150 --> 00:31:26.790
about what's reasonable
and what's possible

00:31:26.790 --> 00:31:29.253
and also take people back to their plans.

00:31:30.570 --> 00:31:32.700
These boards have assured
themselves of plans

00:31:32.700 --> 00:31:33.850
earlier on in the year.

00:31:35.130 --> 00:31:37.020
And again, an earlier
conversation this week

00:31:37.020 --> 00:31:37.853
with the chief exec

00:31:37.853 --> 00:31:40.770
was going through exactly
that challenge in ED.

00:31:40.770 --> 00:31:44.670
Some leadership challenges
with the clinical leadership

00:31:44.670 --> 00:31:46.500
in that organisation

00:31:46.500 --> 00:31:47.700
thought it was wise

00:31:47.700 --> 00:31:50.097
to have a proper warts-and-all
board conversation,

00:31:50.097 --> 00:31:51.247
and I agreed with them.

00:31:52.350 --> 00:31:54.400
And they're going to facilitate that now.

00:31:55.290 --> 00:31:57.270
But also, I think, if we're honest,

00:31:57.270 --> 00:31:59.610
try not to get caught too much up

00:31:59.610 --> 00:32:02.100
in the "it's either this or that,"

00:32:02.100 --> 00:32:06.000
because that's the problem
we've had in recent years.

00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:07.290
We can't have this or that.

00:32:07.290 --> 00:32:09.660
We've actually got to try and
deliver across the whole range

00:32:09.660 --> 00:32:11.020
as best we can.

00:32:11.020 --> 00:32:12.180
- Okay, so we've got...

00:32:12.180 --> 00:32:13.740
Glen wants to come in and then Elizabeth,

00:32:13.740 --> 00:32:15.300
but just before that, I might just

00:32:15.300 --> 00:32:17.583
add a bit of a question myself to it.

00:32:21.210 --> 00:32:23.610
We have produced a lot
of data and analysis

00:32:23.610 --> 00:32:24.960
here at the centre,

00:32:24.960 --> 00:32:27.840
whether it's for ICBs
through the model system work

00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:29.820
or whether it's through trusts

00:32:29.820 --> 00:32:31.890
through the model hospital work

00:32:31.890 --> 00:32:34.410
and the different
benchmarking packs, and so on.

00:32:34.410 --> 00:32:36.260
So there is a lot of data out there

00:32:36.260 --> 00:32:37.740
to help inform trusts

00:32:37.740 --> 00:32:38.970
on where there are opportunities

00:32:38.970 --> 00:32:40.970
to improve their performance, and so on.

00:32:42.210 --> 00:32:46.260
Recognise, you know, it's only
one piece of crunching data

00:32:46.260 --> 00:32:47.400
so it's not necessarily the gospel,

00:32:47.400 --> 00:32:49.440
but it's got some really
great information in there.

00:32:49.440 --> 00:32:53.070
To what degree are trusts
and ICBs using that

00:32:53.070 --> 00:32:56.317
to identify almost the answer
to your question, Andrew:

00:32:56.317 --> 00:32:57.237
"Where should they be focusing?

00:32:57.237 --> 00:32:58.470
And where should they be going?

00:32:58.470 --> 00:32:59.940
So I don't know whether, Glen,

00:32:59.940 --> 00:33:01.470
you want to come in on that, or Elizabeth.

00:33:01.470 --> 00:33:03.106
- Could maybe add that to
what I was going to say.

00:33:03.106 --> 00:33:07.500
I mean, so, the four boards
in the foundation group,

00:33:07.500 --> 00:33:09.960
we went through all of
that data when we last met.

00:33:09.960 --> 00:33:13.530
And it highlights always
opportunities for improvement,

00:33:13.530 --> 00:33:17.160
no matter how well you're
doing on some of those things.

00:33:17.160 --> 00:33:19.487
Andrew, you mentioned the chief
exec job being a great job.

00:33:19.487 --> 00:33:21.150
One of the reasons why it's a great job

00:33:21.150 --> 00:33:22.320
is because it's quite challenging

00:33:22.320 --> 00:33:23.850
but then really rewarding

00:33:23.850 --> 00:33:28.850
when you can bring that all
together to work for patients.

00:33:28.980 --> 00:33:31.950
The UHB one is a really good example.

00:33:31.950 --> 00:33:33.260
Listened to their chief executive talk

00:33:33.260 --> 00:33:35.850
at the West Midlands chief
execs forum couple of weeks ago

00:33:35.850 --> 00:33:38.580
about what they've done,
and what they've done there

00:33:38.580 --> 00:33:41.460
is they reengineered a
lot of their pathways,

00:33:41.460 --> 00:33:43.350
and they've innovated,

00:33:43.350 --> 00:33:45.030
so it's not just about working harder

00:33:45.030 --> 00:33:46.380
to deliver improved waiting times.

00:33:46.380 --> 00:33:47.820
They work more efficiently to do it,

00:33:47.820 --> 00:33:49.260
so they're a really good example

00:33:49.260 --> 00:33:51.330
of what you can do
using that kind of data.

00:33:51.330 --> 00:33:52.305
- Yeah, very good thanks.

00:33:52.305 --> 00:33:54.570
It'll be good to just perhaps try

00:33:54.570 --> 00:33:56.130
and capture that a bit more and share it.

00:33:56.130 --> 00:33:56.963
Elizabeth?

00:33:56.963 --> 00:33:58.980
- I'd just echo a point,
so I won't labour it.

00:33:58.980 --> 00:34:02.070
In respect of the productivity
data in particular,

00:34:02.070 --> 00:34:03.300
the amount of engagement

00:34:03.300 --> 00:34:05.730
and, I think I've
mentioned before, curiosity

00:34:05.730 --> 00:34:07.650
from boards, not just execs,

00:34:07.650 --> 00:34:09.750
has been huge.

00:34:09.750 --> 00:34:11.700
We've been out and
about to all regions now

00:34:11.700 --> 00:34:13.350
doing various sessions.

00:34:13.350 --> 00:34:16.170
I know, David, you mentioned
some of the slide packs

00:34:16.170 --> 00:34:18.930
and how it's provoking a
different type of conversation,

00:34:18.930 --> 00:34:21.267
because it can't just be "work harder."

00:34:22.560 --> 00:34:23.820
And that's where, I think, the excitement

00:34:23.820 --> 00:34:28.740
from facing into the 10
Year Health Plan is coming,

00:34:28.740 --> 00:34:31.080
because it's got to be about
how do we reform our way

00:34:31.080 --> 00:34:32.670
out of the problem.

00:34:32.670 --> 00:34:36.990
Also, it's hard to answer
the question, because...

00:34:36.990 --> 00:34:40.050
It's hard to answer the
question in a universal way,

00:34:40.050 --> 00:34:41.610
because what I've experienced is,

00:34:41.610 --> 00:34:44.490
people that come to me with the
challenges you've described,

00:34:44.490 --> 00:34:46.590
their issues are so local

00:34:46.590 --> 00:34:49.620
but we are helping them
work their way through it.

00:34:49.620 --> 00:34:52.980
But just the true-up
exercise has told us a lot

00:34:52.980 --> 00:34:55.620
about how we're going to
improve planning this year,

00:34:55.620 --> 00:34:57.990
because we have got
boards across the country

00:34:57.990 --> 00:35:00.030
that don't seem to have activity plans

00:35:00.030 --> 00:35:01.890
that are triangulated well.

00:35:01.890 --> 00:35:03.600
People think they're commissioning.

00:35:03.600 --> 00:35:05.010
And then when you're trying

00:35:05.010 --> 00:35:08.163
to have, you know, sensible
conversations to really help,

00:35:09.210 --> 00:35:10.470
that information isn't there.

00:35:10.470 --> 00:35:12.720
So it's a lot of work that's
gone on through the summer

00:35:12.720 --> 00:35:14.460
just to make sure that we're not

00:35:14.460 --> 00:35:15.690
in this predicament next year.

00:35:15.690 --> 00:35:18.150
- Okay, good. Rob?

00:35:18.150 --> 00:35:18.983
- Yeah, thank you, Chair.

00:35:18.983 --> 00:35:20.680
Think it's just worth pointing out

00:35:21.540 --> 00:35:24.322
in terms of all the good
things we're hearing about UHB

00:35:24.322 --> 00:35:26.640
it's actually been their
clinical leadership

00:35:26.640 --> 00:35:29.970
that has helped to drive the
neighbourhood development

00:35:29.970 --> 00:35:31.410
in the east of Birmingham.

00:35:31.410 --> 00:35:33.750
I was key part of that the last few years.

00:35:33.750 --> 00:35:35.880
We simply couldn't have done it

00:35:35.880 --> 00:35:37.470
without them heavily leaning in,

00:35:37.470 --> 00:35:40.890
both with the GP, social care,
voluntary sector, community,

00:35:40.890 --> 00:35:42.840
but their presence was
absolutely important.

00:35:42.840 --> 00:35:44.880
So it's an example

00:35:44.880 --> 00:35:47.310
where we can both deliver future change

00:35:47.310 --> 00:35:49.770
and deliver the stuff we need to do today.

00:35:49.770 --> 00:35:51.720
- Good. Good focus on Birmingham today.

00:35:53.070 --> 00:35:54.463
So Sarah-Jane, winter.

00:35:54.463 --> 00:35:56.410
- No, I went with the
Black Country accent-

00:35:56.410 --> 00:35:57.243
- Exactly.

00:35:57.243 --> 00:35:58.980
- not the Birmingham one.

00:35:58.980 --> 00:36:00.150
Sorry to disappoint.

00:36:00.150 --> 00:36:01.053
Okay, thank you.

00:36:01.053 --> 00:36:02.940
I'm, obviously, building on the context

00:36:02.940 --> 00:36:05.040
that Jim has set out,

00:36:05.040 --> 00:36:07.950
so I'll just walk us through
the highlights of the paper

00:36:07.950 --> 00:36:09.600
but assume you'll want to focus the time

00:36:09.600 --> 00:36:10.770
on the discussion, Penny.

00:36:10.770 --> 00:36:12.480
So, we're going into this winter

00:36:12.480 --> 00:36:16.200
in a challenging context operationally.

00:36:16.200 --> 00:36:17.130
I think the main measure

00:36:17.130 --> 00:36:20.160
that we always look at
across the ops teams,

00:36:20.160 --> 00:36:21.860
not just in NHS England but in the NHS,

00:36:21.860 --> 00:36:23.220
is the bed occupancy

00:36:23.220 --> 00:36:25.740
because the bed occupancy
really drives our ability

00:36:25.740 --> 00:36:28.380
to be able to maintain system flow,

00:36:28.380 --> 00:36:32.040
and bed occupancy has been
challenged throughout the year.

00:36:32.040 --> 00:36:33.120
I would, though, say

00:36:33.120 --> 00:36:37.020
that, on the resilience front,
we are going into this winter

00:36:37.020 --> 00:36:38.850
with a more resilient ambulance service

00:36:38.850 --> 00:36:40.410
than we have previously.

00:36:40.410 --> 00:36:43.443
We've done some really good
work on ambulance productivity,

00:36:44.520 --> 00:36:45.810
different ways of working,

00:36:45.810 --> 00:36:48.450
routing into community
services, and so on.

00:36:48.450 --> 00:36:50.940
And our ambulances are
routinely now running

00:36:50.940 --> 00:36:53.550
around 10 minutes ahead of
where they were previously,

00:36:53.550 --> 00:36:55.380
so that is people with
heart attack / strokes

00:36:55.380 --> 00:37:00.380
getting collected and taken to
hospital 10 minutes earlier.

00:37:00.510 --> 00:37:02.790
So real challenges in the hospital sector

00:37:02.790 --> 00:37:04.470
as we approach winter.

00:37:04.470 --> 00:37:06.540
Slightly more resilient ambulance service.

00:37:06.540 --> 00:37:08.280
I know it'll still come under pressure.

00:37:08.280 --> 00:37:12.000
So, the strategy this year's
had three phases to it:

00:37:12.000 --> 00:37:14.070
first of all, preparation.

00:37:14.070 --> 00:37:15.630
So, in terms of preparation,

00:37:15.630 --> 00:37:20.130
both ICBs and trusts
have collectively worked,

00:37:20.130 --> 00:37:23.040
including with colleagues from
primary care, social care,

00:37:23.040 --> 00:37:25.080
on what their winter plans are.

00:37:25.080 --> 00:37:26.340
We've tested them

00:37:26.340 --> 00:37:29.520
against three different
scenario pressure levels

00:37:29.520 --> 00:37:31.530
so that everybody's really clear

00:37:31.530 --> 00:37:33.960
who needs to do what when;

00:37:33.960 --> 00:37:34.830
what we might want to do

00:37:34.830 --> 00:37:36.630
in terms of sort of alternatives

00:37:36.630 --> 00:37:38.850
to admission conveyance, and so on;

00:37:38.850 --> 00:37:40.710
when capacity needs to open;

00:37:40.710 --> 00:37:44.580
importantly, how IPC needs
to work at the front door;

00:37:44.580 --> 00:37:46.530
the importance of vaccination rates,

00:37:46.530 --> 00:37:47.730
both in the population

00:37:47.730 --> 00:37:52.590
but also for our clinical
teams and wider staff groups.

00:37:52.590 --> 00:37:54.060
We tested those plans formally.

00:37:54.060 --> 00:37:56.613
In September, I reported
that back to the board.

00:37:57.707 --> 00:38:01.650
There were some gaps identified
in both trust and ICB level,

00:38:01.650 --> 00:38:02.730
but through the regions,

00:38:02.730 --> 00:38:05.070
we worked hard with those organisations

00:38:05.070 --> 00:38:07.980
to close the gaps as best we could,

00:38:07.980 --> 00:38:09.660
but we still knew, as winter approach,

00:38:09.660 --> 00:38:12.120
that we would have some challenges.

00:38:12.120 --> 00:38:15.000
We then moved into what we've
described as staying ahead.

00:38:15.000 --> 00:38:17.850
So, always, when you're
under operational pressure,

00:38:17.850 --> 00:38:19.530
we don't want to hit the stage

00:38:19.530 --> 00:38:20.880
where some of the things

00:38:20.880 --> 00:38:22.590
that are absolutely safety-critical

00:38:22.590 --> 00:38:24.900
really struggle to be able to function.

00:38:24.900 --> 00:38:27.030
So the staying-ahead strategy is really

00:38:27.030 --> 00:38:29.670
about having a very close focus now

00:38:29.670 --> 00:38:31.770
on discharge and bed occupancy,

00:38:31.770 --> 00:38:34.710
not letting too much pressure
build up at the front door,

00:38:34.710 --> 00:38:36.750
not ending up in a situation

00:38:36.750 --> 00:38:38.580
where we've got too many
people in corridors,

00:38:38.580 --> 00:38:40.830
which means we can't get
people off back of ambulances,

00:38:40.830 --> 00:38:43.530
and I'll come back to
corridors in a moment.

00:38:43.530 --> 00:38:45.610
And then when we really get up against it,

00:38:45.610 --> 00:38:47.460
it is that operational response.

00:38:47.460 --> 00:38:50.190
I think every winter forever in the NHS

00:38:50.190 --> 00:38:52.050
there's been a two-to-three-week period

00:38:52.050 --> 00:38:54.840
where it is really, really pressurised,

00:38:54.840 --> 00:38:56.580
and it's about how we all work together

00:38:56.580 --> 00:38:57.660
up and down the system,

00:38:57.660 --> 00:38:59.400
and it is 24-7

00:38:59.400 --> 00:39:01.590
to make sure that we take the decisions

00:39:01.590 --> 00:39:03.060
to spread the clinical risks

00:39:03.060 --> 00:39:04.680
so that, in an attempt

00:39:04.680 --> 00:39:07.080
to not work collectively with the system,

00:39:07.080 --> 00:39:09.120
we don't end up shifting
the risk onto patients,

00:39:09.120 --> 00:39:12.510
which is what we need
to avoid at all costs.

00:39:12.510 --> 00:39:15.210
So as we go into this more
challenging period now,

00:39:15.210 --> 00:39:17.610
our three main priorities will be

00:39:17.610 --> 00:39:21.690
to be able to maintain the
Category 2 ambulance response.

00:39:21.690 --> 00:39:23.730
We cannot leave people at home unattended

00:39:23.730 --> 00:39:25.230
that need an ambulance.

00:39:25.230 --> 00:39:28.140
To do that, we need to
maintain hospital handover

00:39:28.140 --> 00:39:29.400
at the front door,

00:39:29.400 --> 00:39:30.930
and to do that, we will end up

00:39:30.930 --> 00:39:33.240
with some of our A&amp;E
departments overcrowded

00:39:33.240 --> 00:39:34.620
and some patients in corridors,

00:39:34.620 --> 00:39:37.710
which we know is unacceptable.

00:39:37.710 --> 00:39:40.320
But I think the clinical...

00:39:40.320 --> 00:39:41.760
The clinicians who lead the discussions

00:39:41.760 --> 00:39:44.640
are very clear that the
most significant risk of all

00:39:44.640 --> 00:39:46.770
is to not respond with an ambulance

00:39:46.770 --> 00:39:47.880
to someone who needs one,

00:39:47.880 --> 00:39:51.363
so that always will have to
be our strategic priority.

00:39:52.260 --> 00:39:53.940
In terms of the corridor care, then,

00:39:53.940 --> 00:39:56.520
it is an horrendous consequence

00:39:56.520 --> 00:39:59.667
of the situation that
we find ourselves in,

00:39:59.667 --> 00:40:04.470
and we know, for individual
patients, this is horrendous.

00:40:04.470 --> 00:40:07.470
We're working really
close with NHS England

00:40:07.470 --> 00:40:08.670
through the regional teams

00:40:08.670 --> 00:40:09.563
with some of the organisations

00:40:09.563 --> 00:40:12.600
that've got the biggest challenges.

00:40:12.600 --> 00:40:14.730
Again, to return to the
conversation we had before,

00:40:14.730 --> 00:40:16.830
there are some things
that organisations can do

00:40:16.830 --> 00:40:19.410
that are within their own
gift to help to resolve it.

00:40:19.410 --> 00:40:22.050
Some of them are wider system
things that will be driven

00:40:22.050 --> 00:40:25.740
by neighbourhood health and
community response, and so on.

00:40:25.740 --> 00:40:27.660
We do now have a team,

00:40:27.660 --> 00:40:30.281
led by Professor Tim Briggs through GIRFT,

00:40:30.281 --> 00:40:32.543
that is working more closely
with the organisations

00:40:32.543 --> 00:40:34.770
that've got the really big challenges

00:40:34.770 --> 00:40:36.000
to see what more we can do,

00:40:36.000 --> 00:40:37.410
particularly over the next few weeks,

00:40:37.410 --> 00:40:39.030
to be able to resolve that.

00:40:39.030 --> 00:40:40.557
But some of these are longstanding issues,

00:40:40.557 --> 00:40:43.050
and we know hospitals
would be under pressure,

00:40:43.050 --> 00:40:44.250
and we're exceptionally grateful

00:40:44.250 --> 00:40:46.830
to the clinicians that are
working in these circumstances

00:40:46.830 --> 00:40:49.860
to maintain safety.

00:40:49.860 --> 00:40:51.810
Finally, Penny, I just
want to say a few words now

00:40:51.810 --> 00:40:53.640
about the interface with industrial action

00:40:53.640 --> 00:40:57.540
and why we, in the operational
team, are more concerned

00:40:57.540 --> 00:40:59.310
about this period of industrial action

00:40:59.310 --> 00:41:01.260
than we have been about previous ones,

00:41:01.260 --> 00:41:03.330
given we've led each

00:41:03.330 --> 00:41:05.520
of the periods that
we've unfortunately had

00:41:05.520 --> 00:41:07.500
over the last few years.

00:41:07.500 --> 00:41:09.270
So the industrial action falls

00:41:09.270 --> 00:41:11.130
in a challenging time for us

00:41:11.130 --> 00:41:12.540
in the run up to Christmas,

00:41:12.540 --> 00:41:15.090
when we've got the high bed occupancy,

00:41:15.090 --> 00:41:17.193
the rising levels of flu, and so on.

00:41:18.420 --> 00:41:21.060
We will be able to
maintain through the period

00:41:21.060 --> 00:41:23.880
because we'll use our
consultant colleagues

00:41:23.880 --> 00:41:24.830
to cover the shifts

00:41:26.290 --> 00:41:28.950
that are left vacant by striking doctors.

00:41:28.950 --> 00:41:31.830
But when the period of
industrial action finishes,

00:41:31.830 --> 00:41:34.230
we've only then got two short days

00:41:34.230 --> 00:41:36.150
before we go into four days

00:41:36.150 --> 00:41:38.850
of bank holiday and weekend working.

00:41:38.850 --> 00:41:40.060
And when we emerge from that,

00:41:40.060 --> 00:41:42.120
we're then in the most challenging week

00:41:42.120 --> 00:41:44.040
that the NHS faces every year

00:41:44.040 --> 00:41:47.580
because that always happens
between Christmas and New Year.

00:41:47.580 --> 00:41:49.113
So we can't think about this

00:41:49.113 --> 00:41:50.940
as a five-day strike period,

00:41:50.940 --> 00:41:52.770
from a resilience perspective.

00:41:52.770 --> 00:41:54.150
We have to think about it

00:41:54.150 --> 00:41:56.580
over that two-to-three-week period,

00:41:56.580 --> 00:41:58.170
and it is the same people

00:41:58.170 --> 00:42:00.240
that we would normally call on

00:42:00.240 --> 00:42:02.340
to do the extra shifts

00:42:02.340 --> 00:42:04.260
in the gaps between Christmas and New Year

00:42:04.260 --> 00:42:06.870
that will have worked to
have covered the strikes.

00:42:06.870 --> 00:42:09.750
We also know that we get a bit of an issue

00:42:09.750 --> 00:42:11.910
in the way that our
discharge processes work.

00:42:11.910 --> 00:42:15.540
A lot of them get brought
forward during industrial action,

00:42:15.540 --> 00:42:17.280
which means that bed occupancy can

00:42:17.280 --> 00:42:19.710
rise quite steeply afterwards.

00:42:19.710 --> 00:42:21.960
So I think we're going into this one

00:42:21.960 --> 00:42:24.480
really quite concerned about our ability,

00:42:24.480 --> 00:42:28.380
not necessarily to manage
through it but to manage post it.

00:42:28.380 --> 00:42:30.570
And I think that's why Jim referenced

00:42:30.570 --> 00:42:32.160
at the end of his opening

00:42:32.160 --> 00:42:33.000
that we need to think

00:42:33.000 --> 00:42:35.760
if there's anything more
we can do, as NHS England,

00:42:35.760 --> 00:42:38.070
to support our frontline leaders,

00:42:38.070 --> 00:42:39.260
who are doing everything they can

00:42:39.260 --> 00:42:41.580
to think through how they manage this.

00:42:41.580 --> 00:42:44.670
I met a group of 20 this week.

00:42:44.670 --> 00:42:47.520
Lots of concern, the
most concern I've seen.

00:42:47.520 --> 00:42:49.230
But we will work together

00:42:49.230 --> 00:42:52.767
to do the best we can over
the period for patients.

00:42:52.767 --> 00:42:53.600
Thank you.
- And then Jim, do you want

00:42:53.600 --> 00:42:54.776
to come back on that?
- Yeah, just to add to that,

00:42:54.776 --> 00:42:56.160
there's a really tricky balance here

00:42:56.160 --> 00:43:00.637
where we have to recognise
the amount of risk out there,

00:43:00.637 --> 00:43:03.090
and there certainly are
pockets of the country

00:43:03.090 --> 00:43:05.340
that are in enhanced state

00:43:05.340 --> 00:43:07.980
of escalation, clinical
incidents, et cetera.

00:43:07.980 --> 00:43:10.760
And that will get worse
over the next few weeks,

00:43:10.760 --> 00:43:12.423
so in lots of ways, it'll
feel like the COVID period

00:43:12.423 --> 00:43:15.960
in, you know, the kind of
pressure that people are facing.

00:43:15.960 --> 00:43:18.330
So we need to be able
to do things that help,

00:43:18.330 --> 00:43:21.720
but the feedback
Sarah-Jane's had this week,

00:43:21.720 --> 00:43:23.040
which I thought was really helpful,

00:43:23.040 --> 00:43:26.040
and it's helpful to have
the conversation, isn't it,

00:43:26.040 --> 00:43:28.350
is about if that help is
we just have lots of calls

00:43:28.350 --> 00:43:30.270
with people who are actually trying

00:43:30.270 --> 00:43:33.630
to manage risk in their organisation,

00:43:33.630 --> 00:43:34.560
that's not helpful.

00:43:34.560 --> 00:43:35.900
So we've just got to
get that balance right

00:43:35.900 --> 00:43:36.960
in the next couple of weeks

00:43:36.960 --> 00:43:39.210
about, genuinely, what can we do

00:43:39.210 --> 00:43:42.720
to introduce support, better decisions,

00:43:42.720 --> 00:43:45.960
different risk thresholds
to reflect the circumstances

00:43:45.960 --> 00:43:47.400
but not add to the burden

00:43:47.400 --> 00:43:48.807
of people out there on the front line.

00:43:48.807 --> 00:43:50.730
It's quite a hard balance.

00:43:50.730 --> 00:43:52.620
- Yeah, and just on that;

00:43:52.620 --> 00:43:54.750
then we come back to wider things;

00:43:54.750 --> 00:43:58.140
I mean, are there other things
that we should be considering

00:43:58.140 --> 00:43:59.940
in the same way that we did in COVID

00:43:59.940 --> 00:44:03.180
so, for example, flexing
some of our staffing models,

00:44:03.180 --> 00:44:05.850
looking at greater use of technology?

00:44:05.850 --> 00:44:08.220
- Absolutely, all that's
in the mix, Penny.

00:44:08.220 --> 00:44:10.770
So we are dusting all of that off again

00:44:10.770 --> 00:44:12.870
and thinking about what more can we do,

00:44:12.870 --> 00:44:15.030
what more can we do about
discharge, for example,

00:44:15.030 --> 00:44:17.580
or reduce occupancy as we're
going to this, as well.

00:44:17.580 --> 00:44:19.070
And that will require us all

00:44:19.070 --> 00:44:22.500
to work together on risk
thresholds and stay together, yeah,

00:44:22.500 --> 00:44:24.030
because not all of it'll work,

00:44:24.030 --> 00:44:27.600
but what we can't do is
just watch it happen.

00:44:27.600 --> 00:44:30.750
- Yeah, okay. So, I've
got couple of questions.

00:44:30.750 --> 00:44:31.800
I know Sam wants to come in.

00:44:31.800 --> 00:44:33.480
Maybe we'll take a few together, Andrew.

00:44:33.480 --> 00:44:35.640
So, mine were two,

00:44:35.640 --> 00:44:38.430
and I'm linking then to
the performance report.

00:44:38.430 --> 00:44:42.840
So, the performance report
page 21 would suggest

00:44:42.840 --> 00:44:47.133
that we're not doing
very well on discharge,

00:44:48.300 --> 00:44:51.480
and I worry about that
because our length of stay

00:44:51.480 --> 00:44:53.220
is a bit of an outlier globally,

00:44:53.220 --> 00:44:55.263
the fact that it's going up,

00:44:56.520 --> 00:45:00.207
and we know it's gone up
since pre COVID, and so on.

00:45:00.207 --> 00:45:02.460
And we do hear quite different things.

00:45:02.460 --> 00:45:03.900
You know, partly, we could sort this

00:45:03.900 --> 00:45:06.540
by improving processes
within our organisations.

00:45:06.540 --> 00:45:11.520
Partly, this is about improving capacity

00:45:11.520 --> 00:45:12.420
outside of hospital.

00:45:12.420 --> 00:45:13.460
But then you speak to the suppliers

00:45:13.460 --> 00:45:14.940
of that capacity outside of hospital,

00:45:14.940 --> 00:45:16.470
and they say that they've
got the capacity there,

00:45:16.470 --> 00:45:17.610
that we're not using it,

00:45:17.610 --> 00:45:20.790
so it'd be quite good to
have a bit more on discharge.

00:45:20.790 --> 00:45:22.680
And then also on the performance report,

00:45:22.680 --> 00:45:23.820
we did say we're going to look

00:45:23.820 --> 00:45:26.100
at rates of admission to hospital,

00:45:26.100 --> 00:45:27.043
and I can't see that in there,

00:45:27.043 --> 00:45:29.520
so I think if we could get that in,

00:45:29.520 --> 00:45:31.710
because we do have this very big variation

00:45:31.710 --> 00:45:34.440
in terms of, the same conditions,

00:45:34.440 --> 00:45:37.320
who ends up being admitted
to hospital and who doesn't

00:45:37.320 --> 00:45:38.520
in different parts of the country

00:45:38.520 --> 00:45:40.190
and something we're
spending a lot of time on

00:45:40.190 --> 00:45:41.310
in the neighbourhood work,

00:45:41.310 --> 00:45:44.550
but we need to look at it
through this lens as well.

00:45:44.550 --> 00:45:45.930
Sam?

00:45:45.930 --> 00:45:50.430
- Yeah, mine's on what more
we can do for flu vaccination.

00:45:50.430 --> 00:45:51.780
The latest figures I saw,

00:45:51.780 --> 00:45:53.433
we've done better than last year,

00:45:55.140 --> 00:45:57.760
but having the levels of flu
that we've got at the moment,

00:45:57.760 --> 00:45:59.310
which is intensely high,

00:45:59.310 --> 00:46:03.720
is also an opportunity, actually,
to get people vaccinated

00:46:03.720 --> 00:46:06.990
and also, dare I say,

00:46:06.990 --> 00:46:10.620
if you're hoping for a
possibility of herd immunity,

00:46:10.620 --> 00:46:12.660
which is always a struggle,

00:46:12.660 --> 00:46:15.150
focusing on those ones
not eligible on the NHS.

00:46:15.150 --> 00:46:18.180
In my area, you can get it for 10 pounds.

00:46:18.180 --> 00:46:22.230
That's probably one of the best
investments anyone could do

00:46:22.230 --> 00:46:24.180
in terms of their preventing

00:46:24.180 --> 00:46:25.860
what can be a pretty horrible illness,

00:46:25.860 --> 00:46:27.460
even for somebody who's healthy.

00:46:28.350 --> 00:46:29.183
- [Penny] Andrew.

00:46:29.183 --> 00:46:31.020
- So, I thought the piece this morning

00:46:31.020 --> 00:46:32.220
on Radio 4 was quite good,

00:46:32.220 --> 00:46:36.300
around inappropriate
attendance in an ED setting,

00:46:36.300 --> 00:46:38.950
and I'm just wondering
whether we could step that up.

00:46:40.020 --> 00:46:43.440
And also, just picking up the issue

00:46:43.440 --> 00:46:48.440
around same-day urgent
attenders for GP practises,

00:46:49.200 --> 00:46:51.090
there's no data in the pack on that,

00:46:51.090 --> 00:46:54.780
but I believe that the
position has improved.

00:46:54.780 --> 00:46:55.613
- [Penny] Yep.

00:46:55.613 --> 00:46:59.580
- And I don't know whether
there's any further potential

00:46:59.580 --> 00:47:03.540
to maximise that a little bit more.

00:47:03.540 --> 00:47:06.090
So as we go into the strike period,

00:47:06.090 --> 00:47:08.640
you know, as we blitz the
kind of local communities

00:47:08.640 --> 00:47:10.030
around "only go to ED

00:47:11.160 --> 00:47:12.780
if you've got X, Y, and Z.

00:47:12.780 --> 00:47:15.900
Please go elsewhere if you...

00:47:15.900 --> 00:47:20.820
Use 111 or your local GP
practise as an alternative."

00:47:20.820 --> 00:47:23.310
And okay, it's not going
to affect the patients,

00:47:23.310 --> 00:47:25.290
potentially, you admit.

00:47:25.290 --> 00:47:30.140
But it will help clear the ED
down in terms of congestion.

00:47:31.410 --> 00:47:33.150
- Yeah. Rob?

00:47:33.150 --> 00:47:33.983
- Thank you, Chair.

00:47:33.983 --> 00:47:38.340
Just on that point, we
held a nationwide session

00:47:38.340 --> 00:47:41.880
with the comms leads out
in trusts and providers,

00:47:41.880 --> 00:47:43.433
ICBs, et cetera, yesterday.

00:47:43.433 --> 00:47:44.987
There's a real energy for all of that

00:47:44.987 --> 00:47:46.650
that's been generated locally

00:47:46.650 --> 00:47:49.800
because there are slightly
different solutions in each patch

00:47:49.800 --> 00:47:51.810
and you're seeing a lot of
people wanting to lean in.

00:47:51.810 --> 00:47:56.340
It's not just leaving this to
the acute communications teams

00:47:56.340 --> 00:47:58.620
to deal with the problem
that's in front of them.

00:47:58.620 --> 00:48:01.560
It's mental health community,

00:48:01.560 --> 00:48:03.963
ICB colleagues really
emphasising the options

00:48:03.963 --> 00:48:05.400
that are there for you.

00:48:05.400 --> 00:48:06.233
I think, nationally,

00:48:06.233 --> 00:48:10.363
we've done a "24 Hours Not
in A&amp;E" set of materials

00:48:12.030 --> 00:48:13.920
that I think is going to
help locally, as well,

00:48:13.920 --> 00:48:16.920
but it's very much locally driven,

00:48:16.920 --> 00:48:18.270
what options are available.

00:48:18.270 --> 00:48:19.890
But you got a real sense yesterday

00:48:19.890 --> 00:48:21.840
that the whole system's wanting to lean in

00:48:21.840 --> 00:48:24.390
to help ensure they're doing
everything that they can

00:48:24.390 --> 00:48:27.660
that patients and communities are aware

00:48:27.660 --> 00:48:29.960
of all the choices that
are available to them.

00:48:31.194 --> 00:48:33.870
- Okay, so, piece on discharge.

00:48:33.870 --> 00:48:34.710
- [Mark Bailie] Uh.

00:48:34.710 --> 00:48:35.674
- Oh, sorry, Mark.

00:48:35.674 --> 00:48:38.820
- Sarah-Jane, on that
inappropriate attendance,

00:48:38.820 --> 00:48:42.343
what sort of scale of people are in UEC

00:48:42.343 --> 00:48:45.120
who are either not treated

00:48:45.120 --> 00:48:47.170
or should've been treated somewhere else?

00:48:48.120 --> 00:48:49.350
- I can start there and work back.

00:48:49.350 --> 00:48:50.730
So, we've got an acuity tool.

00:48:50.730 --> 00:48:53.040
It's not completely
rolled out and deployed,

00:48:53.040 --> 00:48:54.867
but it's something that the
clinical team worked on,

00:48:54.867 --> 00:48:57.150
and we did work with
a lot of organisations

00:48:57.150 --> 00:48:58.200
to implement this tool.

00:48:58.200 --> 00:49:02.040
Which, basically, at
the point of attendance,

00:49:02.040 --> 00:49:03.540
there's a triage that takes place

00:49:03.540 --> 00:49:07.210
that puts people in
acuity categories 1 to 5

00:49:08.340 --> 00:49:12.240
with the sort of underlying model

00:49:12.240 --> 00:49:15.180
that the patients that are
in acuity group 4 and 5

00:49:15.180 --> 00:49:16.980
could've been treated elsewhere,

00:49:16.980 --> 00:49:20.310
and about 40% of patients that attend

00:49:20.310 --> 00:49:24.243
would be suitable to be treated
in an alternative setting.

00:49:25.209 --> 00:49:26.449
- [Mark Bailie] Four in 10?

00:49:26.449 --> 00:49:29.370
- Yes, but that alternative setting,

00:49:29.370 --> 00:49:32.220
obviously, needs to be there
and be open for it to happen,

00:49:32.220 --> 00:49:33.152
so it's not...

00:49:33.152 --> 00:49:35.010
It would give somebody
an acuity score of 4

00:49:35.010 --> 00:49:37.350
and say they're suitable for a UTC,

00:49:37.350 --> 00:49:38.850
but it might be 10 o'clock at night,

00:49:38.850 --> 00:49:40.860
and the UTC might not be open.

00:49:40.860 --> 00:49:43.890
But in terms of patients that truly need

00:49:43.890 --> 00:49:47.370
to be in an emergency department,

00:49:47.370 --> 00:49:49.710
that is about the scale
of the opportunity,

00:49:49.710 --> 00:49:52.380
but the community services
would need to look different.

00:49:52.380 --> 00:49:55.830
There is then a group
of that 60% that remain

00:49:55.830 --> 00:49:58.500
that actually could be
fast-tracked really quite quickly

00:49:58.500 --> 00:50:00.000
and are, in many organisations,

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:05.000
to a Same Day Emergency Care
or a frailty type of facility,

00:50:05.040 --> 00:50:08.070
which means that true
emergency department patients

00:50:08.070 --> 00:50:09.420
are probably less than half

00:50:09.420 --> 00:50:12.840
of those that are currently
in many people's departments.

00:50:12.840 --> 00:50:14.190
But like I say, it's not as simple

00:50:14.190 --> 00:50:15.990
as those ones shouldn't be there,

00:50:15.990 --> 00:50:18.240
because those people need a something else

00:50:18.240 --> 00:50:19.863
and it's not always there,

00:50:21.263 --> 00:50:22.170
but we're pretty clear on-
- But luckily, we've got

00:50:22.170 --> 00:50:24.277
a 10-year plan that says
that we're going to...

00:50:24.277 --> 00:50:28.890
- But in the context of
a massive spike in flu,

00:50:28.890 --> 00:50:31.260
that clearly isn't in
the general discourse

00:50:31.260 --> 00:50:32.880
in society today

00:50:32.880 --> 00:50:36.750
and; I'll be polite;
the unfortunate timing

00:50:36.750 --> 00:50:37.983
of industrial action.

00:50:39.090 --> 00:50:43.420
Rob, is there a way
that that scale of issue

00:50:44.670 --> 00:50:46.920
can be shared with the
population as a whole?

00:50:48.180 --> 00:50:49.590
- So, we're going to pick this up, Mark,

00:50:49.590 --> 00:50:51.780
as the whole "what can we do

00:50:51.780 --> 00:50:54.180
in the run into this
round of industrial action

00:50:54.180 --> 00:50:56.580
but also generally round winter?"

00:50:56.580 --> 00:50:58.560
Just to pick up on Sarah-Jane's point...

00:50:58.560 --> 00:50:59.790
And this will sound ridiculous,

00:50:59.790 --> 00:51:03.390
but on days where there's a big
football match, for example,

00:51:03.390 --> 00:51:05.130
A&amp;E footfall massively drops.

00:51:05.130 --> 00:51:06.840
There are other times of the year

00:51:06.840 --> 00:51:08.733
where footfall massively drops.

00:51:09.840 --> 00:51:12.030
There's loads of ways
of looking at this data,

00:51:12.030 --> 00:51:13.620
but if you look at ambulance deliveries,

00:51:13.620 --> 00:51:16.200
most people would think,
"If you're in an ambulance,

00:51:16.200 --> 00:51:18.420
you're almost certainly
going to be admitted."

00:51:18.420 --> 00:51:21.330
It's about 40% of that volume
isn't admitted, as well.

00:51:21.330 --> 00:51:22.920
So there is a lot...

00:51:22.920 --> 00:51:25.650
There's no question there's
a lot for us to go at.

00:51:25.650 --> 00:51:27.150
It's just really hard to do

00:51:27.150 --> 00:51:30.090
while you've got lots of
other things going on.

00:51:30.090 --> 00:51:32.280
But I think we've taken the challenge

00:51:32.280 --> 00:51:33.660
and we've given ourselves the challenge

00:51:33.660 --> 00:51:36.120
about what more can we
do in this next week then

00:51:36.120 --> 00:51:37.500
with these things

00:51:37.500 --> 00:51:39.750
to give ourselves a better chance.

00:51:39.750 --> 00:51:43.740
- But well, crucially, we also
have to get to next winter

00:51:43.740 --> 00:51:45.600
not in this situation again.

00:51:45.600 --> 00:51:47.160
It's been the same conversation

00:51:47.160 --> 00:51:48.540
for many people around this table

00:51:48.540 --> 00:51:50.190
for many, many years,

00:51:50.190 --> 00:51:52.200
and we know that we can and we need

00:51:52.200 --> 00:51:53.453
to redesign the model of care

00:51:53.453 --> 00:51:56.430
so that those alternatives are there.

00:51:56.430 --> 00:51:58.980
- And then just to add
in the productivity packs

00:51:58.980 --> 00:52:00.580
that you've talked about, Penny,

00:52:01.650 --> 00:52:03.840
we've got some estimates;

00:52:03.840 --> 00:52:06.210
they're not high estimates, reasonable;

00:52:06.210 --> 00:52:09.120
that suggests we spend
about half a billion pounds

00:52:09.120 --> 00:52:12.030
on A&amp;E attendances with
no significant treatment

00:52:12.030 --> 00:52:13.200
so investigations,

00:52:13.200 --> 00:52:16.741
and we are working out what
that looks like per provider.

00:52:16.741 --> 00:52:17.613
- So lots of opportunity.

00:52:17.613 --> 00:52:20.670
Now, I'm losing control
of the room slightly.

00:52:20.670 --> 00:52:22.013
So we've got...

00:52:22.013 --> 00:52:23.190
I think we won't be able

00:52:23.190 --> 00:52:24.720
to take a second round
of questions on this,

00:52:24.720 --> 00:52:26.670
because we do need to keep going.

00:52:26.670 --> 00:52:28.050
We can come back to things later.

00:52:28.050 --> 00:52:32.850
So, discharge and then on flu uptake-

00:52:32.850 --> 00:52:33.683
- Yeah, so, on discharge-

00:52:33.683 --> 00:52:34.770
- [Penny] flu vaccination.

00:52:34.770 --> 00:52:37.620
- I think this is probably our
number-one area of concern,

00:52:37.620 --> 00:52:40.470
because it's the number-one
area of opportunity.

00:52:40.470 --> 00:52:42.990
So, I think the way we think
about it is three blocks:

00:52:42.990 --> 00:52:45.240
what hospitals themselves
can absolutely do

00:52:45.240 --> 00:52:47.850
through process engineering
and improvement.

00:52:47.850 --> 00:52:50.700
We have seen a small improvement

00:52:50.700 --> 00:52:53.580
in delays that relate
to hospital processes

00:52:53.580 --> 00:52:55.290
over the last year.

00:52:55.290 --> 00:52:58.530
There are still delays that
relate, but they are coming down

00:52:58.530 --> 00:53:00.240
as a proportion of the total delays,

00:53:00.240 --> 00:53:01.710
and we'll continue to drive that

00:53:01.710 --> 00:53:05.253
through the productivity packs,
the GIRFT team, et cetera.

00:53:06.840 --> 00:53:08.520
There's then a big group, and I think

00:53:08.520 --> 00:53:11.130
this is why you're hearing
what you're hearing, Penny,

00:53:11.130 --> 00:53:14.190
where the delay relates
to the decision-making

00:53:14.190 --> 00:53:17.580
about the process of
moving out of hospital

00:53:17.580 --> 00:53:19.680
into the domiciliary care package

00:53:19.680 --> 00:53:22.830
or the nursing home, et cetera.

00:53:22.830 --> 00:53:25.050
And a lot of that delay actually relates

00:53:25.050 --> 00:53:27.957
to the social worker capacity

00:53:27.957 --> 00:53:30.390
and the trusted assessor model.

00:53:30.390 --> 00:53:33.000
Actually, once patients are assessed,

00:53:33.000 --> 00:53:34.980
they can move into the capacity,

00:53:34.980 --> 00:53:36.090
but we've got a delay there.

00:53:36.090 --> 00:53:38.400
Now, where that's working well,

00:53:38.400 --> 00:53:39.960
there's discharge to assess models.

00:53:39.960 --> 00:53:40.980
There's trusted assessor.

00:53:40.980 --> 00:53:42.750
In places where it's not,

00:53:42.750 --> 00:53:45.630
we can see a delay sometimes
of four or five days

00:53:45.630 --> 00:53:46.803
for that assessment to take place,

00:53:46.803 --> 00:53:48.810
so I think that's why
you hear the disconnect

00:53:48.810 --> 00:53:50.730
between the NHS

00:53:50.730 --> 00:53:52.920
saying, "We don't have
enough social care capacity,"

00:53:52.920 --> 00:53:54.247
and social care saying,

00:53:54.247 --> 00:53:55.920
"But we do have the capacity.

00:53:55.920 --> 00:53:58.830
We're just not getting the
referrals through to us

00:53:58.830 --> 00:53:59.910
for the patients."

00:53:59.910 --> 00:54:02.370
So we've got pieces of work
going on on both of those.

00:54:02.370 --> 00:54:04.440
But I think that area in the middle,

00:54:04.440 --> 00:54:06.000
particularly the interface

00:54:06.000 --> 00:54:08.040
between the NHS's own services,

00:54:08.040 --> 00:54:09.930
the hospital and community,

00:54:09.930 --> 00:54:11.460
and then the community and social care,

00:54:11.460 --> 00:54:12.570
is the one where we've got a lot

00:54:12.570 --> 00:54:15.990
of opportunity to go at moving forwards.

00:54:15.990 --> 00:54:18.060
In the here and now in winter,

00:54:18.060 --> 00:54:20.790
we will focus on the
hospital ones on the basis

00:54:20.790 --> 00:54:22.290
that we know the levers,

00:54:22.290 --> 00:54:23.610
we know the things that work,

00:54:23.610 --> 00:54:26.400
we've got a team that's
able to help and support,

00:54:26.400 --> 00:54:27.750
so that's where we are now,

00:54:27.750 --> 00:54:29.280
but there's a lot more to go at.

00:54:29.280 --> 00:54:34.020
I would say that some of our
length-of-stay stabilisation

00:54:34.020 --> 00:54:35.550
is also being driven by a lot

00:54:35.550 --> 00:54:38.160
of patients going through
SDEC pathways now,

00:54:38.160 --> 00:54:40.800
and we must continue to to do that.

00:54:40.800 --> 00:54:42.630
There's a hell of a lot
more opportunity there.

00:54:42.630 --> 00:54:46.440
And then finally, on vaccination,
the channels are all open.

00:54:46.440 --> 00:54:48.030
I think the Flu Finder service.

00:54:48.030 --> 00:54:50.130
There's a lot more convenient locations

00:54:50.130 --> 00:54:52.800
that people can go to at different times,

00:54:52.800 --> 00:54:53.850
all those things.

00:54:53.850 --> 00:54:56.760
I just think we've seen a
bit of a drying up of...

00:54:56.760 --> 00:54:59.913
The people who really wanted
it initially have gone for it,

00:55:00.836 --> 00:55:03.360
and there's been a lot of
campaigns locally and nationally

00:55:03.360 --> 00:55:05.580
to try and push more people through.

00:55:05.580 --> 00:55:08.310
I do think it would be
helpful here, Chair,

00:55:08.310 --> 00:55:09.960
if we could give the really clear message

00:55:09.960 --> 00:55:11.760
about the effectiveness of this vaccine,

00:55:11.760 --> 00:55:14.430
'cause I think there's been
some things circulating

00:55:14.430 --> 00:55:17.220
that, because of the
different strains of flu,

00:55:17.220 --> 00:55:19.050
maybe it's not as effective,

00:55:19.050 --> 00:55:23.040
but our colleagues in
UKHSA are really clear

00:55:23.040 --> 00:55:25.470
that makes it more important
to get the vaccine.

00:55:25.470 --> 00:55:29.310
It does prevent severe illness
and hospital admission,

00:55:29.310 --> 00:55:31.680
and nobody should be taking
a choice not to get it

00:55:31.680 --> 00:55:33.390
because they think it won't be effective.

00:55:33.390 --> 00:55:34.860
It's more important than ever,

00:55:34.860 --> 00:55:36.870
because of the way it's circulating

00:55:36.870 --> 00:55:39.450
and the protection it provides, to get it.

00:55:39.450 --> 00:55:41.643
So we continue to give out that message,

00:55:42.960 --> 00:55:44.880
and yeah, hopefully, we'll get more people

00:55:44.880 --> 00:55:46.500
to come forward over the next few weeks,

00:55:46.500 --> 00:55:48.000
including our own staff,

00:55:48.000 --> 00:55:52.290
because what we can't have
is people going off sick

00:55:52.290 --> 00:55:55.710
in the areas, you know,
where we're really concerned

00:55:55.710 --> 00:55:59.160
at the same time as we've
got this increased need.

00:55:59.160 --> 00:56:00.990
- Okay, good, so I'm going to
move us onto the next item,

00:56:00.990 --> 00:56:04.020
but key things, Rob to work with UKHSA.

00:56:04.020 --> 00:56:06.630
There should be a massive up.

00:56:06.630 --> 00:56:08.010
You know, in COVID, people rushed

00:56:08.010 --> 00:56:09.690
to get to the vaccine, not everybody.

00:56:09.690 --> 00:56:10.710
We've got people...

00:56:10.710 --> 00:56:13.230
We want people to rush
to get this vaccine,

00:56:13.230 --> 00:56:14.850
so we've got to up the ante on that.

00:56:14.850 --> 00:56:16.950
And then we will keep
coming back to the numbers.

00:56:16.950 --> 00:56:19.980
40% of people in A&amp;Es
didn't need to be there.

00:56:19.980 --> 00:56:21.030
We have got the numbers,

00:56:21.030 --> 00:56:24.930
and it's up to 60% of emergency admissions

00:56:24.930 --> 00:56:26.850
may not've been the
most appropriate place.

00:56:26.850 --> 00:56:31.850
And that one-day-average-per-admission
length of stay,

00:56:32.040 --> 00:56:36.480
or 0.9 excess length of stay, is massive.

00:56:36.480 --> 00:56:37.860
So I think three numbers.

00:56:37.860 --> 00:56:39.240
We keep coming back to three numbers.

00:56:39.240 --> 00:56:40.530
Are they going in the right direction?

00:56:40.530 --> 00:56:42.240
And what do we want them to look like

00:56:42.240 --> 00:56:43.890
for next year and the year after?

00:56:43.890 --> 00:56:44.940
Come back to that.

00:56:44.940 --> 00:56:47.550
Okay, so risk, John.

00:56:47.550 --> 00:56:49.770
- Thank you, yep, so, we're
presenting our summaries

00:56:49.770 --> 00:56:53.100
of the strategic and
operational risk registers here,

00:56:53.100 --> 00:56:55.560
and I'd just like to draw
out three points, if I may.

00:56:55.560 --> 00:56:58.620
So the quality of care risk
has decreased in score slightly

00:56:58.620 --> 00:57:00.660
since the last time we
brought this to the Board,

00:57:00.660 --> 00:57:01.493
and that reflects the mitigations

00:57:01.493 --> 00:57:04.290
that are complete and progressing

00:57:04.290 --> 00:57:07.080
around developing the
National Quality Board,

00:57:07.080 --> 00:57:09.330
the integration of
quality-and-safety considerations

00:57:09.330 --> 00:57:12.900
into the model ICB and
the model region work,

00:57:12.900 --> 00:57:14.640
and the work we've been
doing within the organisation

00:57:14.640 --> 00:57:17.640
to look at quality-governance
structures, as well.

00:57:17.640 --> 00:57:18.720
The second point is

00:57:18.720 --> 00:57:20.340
if you look at the first annex

00:57:20.340 --> 00:57:21.780
that summarises our top risks

00:57:21.780 --> 00:57:22.613
you'll see that each of those

00:57:22.613 --> 00:57:24.960
is now mapped to a board committee

00:57:24.960 --> 00:57:27.450
and has a date diarised now

00:57:27.450 --> 00:57:29.730
for a deep dive in the coming year.

00:57:29.730 --> 00:57:31.530
So, the purpose of that is
for each of those committees

00:57:31.530 --> 00:57:33.240
to be able to get into the detail

00:57:33.240 --> 00:57:34.650
that we don't have time to here

00:57:34.650 --> 00:57:37.440
on causes, impacts, mitigating actions

00:57:37.440 --> 00:57:39.210
and what the trajectory looks like

00:57:39.210 --> 00:57:41.730
to, hopefully, bring that risk score down,

00:57:41.730 --> 00:57:45.000
so we'll be asking executives
and chairs of those committees

00:57:45.000 --> 00:57:48.330
to prioritise those in the
coming months and year.

00:57:48.330 --> 00:57:51.090
And then the third point is
we're continuing to work closely

00:57:51.090 --> 00:57:53.340
with colleagues at the
department on risk management.

00:57:53.340 --> 00:57:56.040
We've got a joint session of
our audit committees next week,

00:57:56.040 --> 00:57:56.873
where we're going to be talking

00:57:56.873 --> 00:57:57.930
about our ambition for that

00:57:57.930 --> 00:57:59.490
and how we're going to get there.

00:57:59.490 --> 00:58:00.780
But what we'd like to be able to do

00:58:00.780 --> 00:58:02.760
is to bring together our risk registers

00:58:02.760 --> 00:58:04.290
or at least the common risks within them

00:58:04.290 --> 00:58:06.090
so that it looks and feels the same

00:58:06.090 --> 00:58:09.090
for those responsible for
mitigating those risks

00:58:09.090 --> 00:58:11.040
in our joint executive teams.

00:58:11.040 --> 00:58:13.620
And we know that some risks will continue

00:58:13.620 --> 00:58:15.210
to be specific to each organisation

00:58:15.210 --> 00:58:17.370
while they still exist
as separate entities,

00:58:17.370 --> 00:58:18.420
but where that's not the case,

00:58:18.420 --> 00:58:19.770
we want to avoid duplication

00:58:19.770 --> 00:58:21.570
and things going through
two separate structures

00:58:21.570 --> 00:58:23.130
and feeling overly onerous,

00:58:23.130 --> 00:58:25.830
so that's our job in the coming months.

00:58:25.830 --> 00:58:26.680
I'll pause there.

00:58:28.080 --> 00:58:29.013
- Any questions?

00:58:30.738 --> 00:58:31.710
Okay, I've got one.

00:58:31.710 --> 00:58:36.710
Why is our target score, 20,

00:58:37.470 --> 00:58:39.480
deep red in three areas?

00:58:39.480 --> 00:58:42.303
Why? Why aren't we more
sort of aspirational?

00:58:43.800 --> 00:58:45.000
- So, I think

00:58:45.000 --> 00:58:46.410
risk owners will have
to answer that question

00:58:46.410 --> 00:58:47.910
on an individual basis,

00:58:47.910 --> 00:58:51.120
but in broad strokes,

00:58:51.120 --> 00:58:53.610
the nature of a lot of these top risks

00:58:53.610 --> 00:58:56.160
is such that it's not
all within our control,

00:58:56.160 --> 00:58:58.890
and in some cases, a lot of
it is not within our control.

00:58:58.890 --> 00:59:01.410
And I think that reflects the level

00:59:01.410 --> 00:59:03.390
that we feel that we can take it to

00:59:03.390 --> 00:59:05.463
through actions within NHS England.

00:59:08.130 --> 00:59:10.980
So I'm happy to take that away
with individual risk owners

00:59:10.980 --> 00:59:12.730
and push a bit further, but I think

00:59:13.918 --> 00:59:15.300
that's where we've been historically

00:59:15.300 --> 00:59:16.750
is that it can feel difficult

00:59:18.358 --> 00:59:20.130
to own all the actions
that we know are needed

00:59:20.130 --> 00:59:21.630
across the whole system and wider

00:59:21.630 --> 00:59:24.030
to bring these down to
a more manageable level.

00:59:24.030 --> 00:59:26.070
- I think that would be a
good thing to come back to,

00:59:26.070 --> 00:59:27.780
toward "so what would it take in order

00:59:27.780 --> 00:59:30.750
to get them to a amber?"

00:59:30.750 --> 00:59:32.700
Anyway, okay, good, right,

00:59:32.700 --> 00:59:36.030
let's come on to the wider
performance and delivery report.

00:59:36.030 --> 00:59:36.863
David.

00:59:36.863 --> 00:59:37.696
- Thank you, Chair.

00:59:38.640 --> 00:59:41.190
Report presented as usual,

00:59:41.190 --> 00:59:43.770
just to say it's broken
up into the six domains.

00:59:43.770 --> 00:59:47.130
We've reflected a lot on the
feedback from Board last time,

00:59:47.130 --> 00:59:49.257
and hopefully, colleagues
will see the changes

00:59:49.257 --> 00:59:51.150
that've been made,

00:59:51.150 --> 00:59:52.800
with particular reference to domain one,

00:59:52.800 --> 00:59:55.290
around health and reducing inequalities.

00:59:55.290 --> 00:59:57.600
We've also dived a lot deeper

00:59:57.600 --> 00:59:59.910
into organisational and ICB performance

00:59:59.910 --> 01:00:01.740
against many of these metrics.

01:00:01.740 --> 01:00:03.150
So if it's okay, Chair,

01:00:03.150 --> 01:00:05.280
what I'll do is just briefly summarise

01:00:05.280 --> 01:00:06.720
the improved areas of performance,

01:00:06.720 --> 01:00:08.280
the challenged areas of performance,

01:00:08.280 --> 01:00:12.510
and then just touch on
the key operational areas.

01:00:12.510 --> 01:00:13.737
My colleagues around the table, of course,

01:00:13.737 --> 01:00:15.150
are the experts in this field,

01:00:15.150 --> 01:00:17.790
but delighted to take questions
on any of the points raised.

01:00:17.790 --> 01:00:20.340
So, firstly, just in terms
of improved performance,

01:00:21.210 --> 01:00:23.730
some good areas of performance,
some of which are marginal,

01:00:23.730 --> 01:00:25.940
but it's important to
recognise the success

01:00:25.940 --> 01:00:27.330
in a number of areas:

01:00:27.330 --> 01:00:30.540
firstly, around the
number of adult inpatients

01:00:30.540 --> 01:00:33.420
who are autistic or have
a learning disability

01:00:33.420 --> 01:00:35.310
has continued to reduce,

01:00:35.310 --> 01:00:38.730
which is a good result

01:00:38.730 --> 01:00:40.500
as an area of a significant focus;

01:00:40.500 --> 01:00:42.540
the percentage of patients conveyed

01:00:42.540 --> 01:00:44.400
to emergency departments by ambulance,

01:00:44.400 --> 01:00:47.310
the point that Sarah-Jane
was picking up earlier.

01:00:47.310 --> 01:00:50.520
The percentage of patients
with a general practise

01:00:50.520 --> 01:00:52.350
with recorded CVD

01:00:52.350 --> 01:00:54.150
when their cholesterol's being managed

01:00:54.150 --> 01:00:56.700
continues to improve care.

01:00:56.700 --> 01:00:58.920
Patients referred to
crisis team management,

01:00:58.920 --> 01:01:00.300
again, within 24 hours

01:01:00.300 --> 01:01:01.890
continues to improve.

01:01:01.890 --> 01:01:03.990
The use of antibiotics
or the prescription of it

01:01:03.990 --> 01:01:05.820
in children between zero and nine

01:01:05.820 --> 01:01:07.680
continues to reduce.

01:01:07.680 --> 01:01:10.020
And we touched on this
earlier in the conversation,

01:01:10.020 --> 01:01:11.130
that percentage of patients

01:01:11.130 --> 01:01:14.550
who perceived ease of contacting GPs,

01:01:14.550 --> 01:01:18.120
it's the highest since
this survey has ever begun.

01:01:18.120 --> 01:01:21.090
And you will see now the
online contact is now perceived

01:01:21.090 --> 01:01:22.980
as the easiest contact method,

01:01:22.980 --> 01:01:25.380
overtaking in-person just marginally,

01:01:25.380 --> 01:01:28.680
but it's the first time
we've seen the dial shift.

01:01:28.680 --> 01:01:29.700
Children and young people

01:01:29.700 --> 01:01:32.430
accessing funded mental health services

01:01:32.430 --> 01:01:33.660
continues to improve.

01:01:33.660 --> 01:01:36.630
And that's been an area
of significant focus,

01:01:36.630 --> 01:01:39.150
as has community mental
health access rate,

01:01:39.150 --> 01:01:40.650
something we've been

01:01:40.650 --> 01:01:42.990
performance-managing
our regions very hard on

01:01:42.990 --> 01:01:45.630
to understand the areas of progress.

01:01:45.630 --> 01:01:48.330
And finally, of course, I
won't touch on the finance

01:01:48.330 --> 01:01:50.400
and steal Elizabeth's thunder afterwards,

01:01:50.400 --> 01:01:53.910
but that remains an area
of improved position.

01:01:53.910 --> 01:01:56.370
So if that was a summary of
where things are improving,

01:01:56.370 --> 01:01:57.510
we, of course, have a number

01:01:57.510 --> 01:01:59.670
of areas where we continue
to remain challenged

01:01:59.670 --> 01:02:01.380
and we're working hard.

01:02:01.380 --> 01:02:03.660
The first, continuing around the issue

01:02:03.660 --> 01:02:04.680
of health inequalities,

01:02:04.680 --> 01:02:08.620
our cervical screening
coverage is slightly lower

01:02:09.570 --> 01:02:10.470
than we would've hoped for

01:02:10.470 --> 01:02:12.330
and where we have seen previously,

01:02:12.330 --> 01:02:14.100
as is MMR uptake,

01:02:14.100 --> 01:02:16.860
particularly for the second
dose for five-year-olds,

01:02:16.860 --> 01:02:19.500
which I know is an area of focus.

01:02:19.500 --> 01:02:21.660
The percentage of patients
treated within cancer

01:02:21.660 --> 01:02:23.580
within 62 days of referral

01:02:23.580 --> 01:02:25.260
is slightly below target,

01:02:25.260 --> 01:02:27.390
and you'll see in the pack the detail

01:02:27.390 --> 01:02:31.830
of organisations and ICB
performance in this area.

01:02:31.830 --> 01:02:34.950
And the percentage of
patients waiting over 52 weeks

01:02:34.950 --> 01:02:36.510
for community services, again,

01:02:36.510 --> 01:02:39.210
is slightly lower than we
would wish to see it at,

01:02:39.210 --> 01:02:41.460
compared to previous months.

01:02:41.460 --> 01:02:44.820
Our four-hour performance
Sarah-Jane I know has picked up,

01:02:44.820 --> 01:02:47.910
but we are marginally
behind where we wish to be,

01:02:47.910 --> 01:02:50.820
but an improved position
on previous years.

01:02:50.820 --> 01:02:52.860
And 12-hour performance, we've continued

01:02:52.860 --> 01:02:56.073
to see a deterioration in our performance.

01:02:56.940 --> 01:02:57.840
We're working hard,

01:02:57.840 --> 01:02:59.610
and I'll come on to
talk about mental health

01:02:59.610 --> 01:03:00.510
further on today,

01:03:00.510 --> 01:03:03.450
but the percentage of
patients with suspected autism

01:03:03.450 --> 01:03:05.310
are continuing to wait longer

01:03:05.310 --> 01:03:07.110
than we wish and over 13 weeks,

01:03:07.110 --> 01:03:10.020
but that is an area of significant focus.

01:03:10.020 --> 01:03:15.020
Just finally, the key
operational areas of performance,

01:03:15.150 --> 01:03:17.280
some of these are marginal,
but again, it's important

01:03:17.280 --> 01:03:18.930
to celebrate some of the real success,

01:03:18.930 --> 01:03:21.840
and Jim has picked up on
a number of these already.

01:03:21.840 --> 01:03:25.260
As noted in the news, our
emergency departments continue

01:03:25.260 --> 01:03:27.660
to remain busier than ever.

01:03:27.660 --> 01:03:29.460
Demand on our services for ED

01:03:29.460 --> 01:03:31.290
has risen by over 1,000 a day

01:03:31.290 --> 01:03:33.150
from the same month in the previous year,

01:03:33.150 --> 01:03:36.120
just to put it into perspective.

01:03:36.120 --> 01:03:37.980
In October 2025,

01:03:37.980 --> 01:03:40.650
which is where we're
working to at the moment,

01:03:40.650 --> 01:03:43.620
about 74.1 of our patients

01:03:43.620 --> 01:03:45.540
were treated within the four hours

01:03:45.540 --> 01:03:47.160
that we've picked up earlier.

01:03:47.160 --> 01:03:49.020
And we've seen some challenges

01:03:49.020 --> 01:03:51.570
around the ambulance Cat 2 times,

01:03:51.570 --> 01:03:54.360
which Sarah-Jane was referencing earlier.

01:03:54.360 --> 01:03:56.370
In terms of our overall waiting list,

01:03:56.370 --> 01:03:58.920
as Jim said, we are
waiting for final stats,

01:03:58.920 --> 01:04:02.460
but if we look at the end of
September, it's pleasing to see

01:04:02.460 --> 01:04:04.890
that there was an improvement
in our overall waiting list.

01:04:04.890 --> 01:04:08.280
Back down to 7.39 million people waiting,

01:04:08.280 --> 01:04:11.483
so that's a decrease of nearly 16,000

01:04:11.483 --> 01:04:14.070
and 61, nearly 62, percent of patients

01:04:14.070 --> 01:04:15.780
waiting less than 18 weeks,

01:04:15.780 --> 01:04:20.670
against our end-of-March
ambition next year of 65%.

01:04:20.670 --> 01:04:22.470
Two final statistics, if I may.

01:04:22.470 --> 01:04:24.413
The first is

01:04:24.413 --> 01:04:28.990
that of September we had 286,000 people

01:04:30.210 --> 01:04:34.170
being seen for urgent
suspected-cancer referral,

01:04:34.170 --> 01:04:35.640
so just to put that in perspective,

01:04:35.640 --> 01:04:38.910
there's about 13 1/2
thousand patients every day

01:04:38.910 --> 01:04:41.100
now being seen for
urgent suspected cancer,

01:04:41.100 --> 01:04:43.200
and a lot of those
pathways radically improved

01:04:43.200 --> 01:04:45.060
from where they were previously.

01:04:45.060 --> 01:04:46.740
And very finally, performance

01:04:46.740 --> 01:04:50.070
against the 28-day for faster diagnostics

01:04:50.070 --> 01:04:53.850
is at around 74% so not too far away

01:04:53.850 --> 01:04:57.780
from the constitutional standard
that we wish to achieve.

01:04:57.780 --> 01:05:00.510
Very happy to take comments
on anything in the pack,

01:05:00.510 --> 01:05:04.290
and, as I say, the experts
also, around the table, Penny.

01:05:04.290 --> 01:05:08.793
- Right. I've got a few
while everyone's thinking.

01:05:09.855 --> 01:05:10.900
- [John] Got my pen out.

01:05:10.900 --> 01:05:12.093
- First is comment.

01:05:13.410 --> 01:05:15.480
Well, they're both comments,
really, so on the first one,

01:05:15.480 --> 01:05:17.010
I'm pretty sure,

01:05:17.010 --> 01:05:19.110
but I may, of course, have got this wrong,

01:05:19.110 --> 01:05:20.700
that we said we would separate out

01:05:20.700 --> 01:05:22.413
effectiveness from experience.

01:05:23.310 --> 01:05:24.870
I don't think we can put the two together.

01:05:24.870 --> 01:05:27.507
They're completely different.
They're different focuses.

01:05:27.507 --> 01:05:29.790
And I think if Louise was
here she would want us

01:05:29.790 --> 01:05:33.820
to see a dedicated section
on patient user experience.

01:05:33.820 --> 01:05:35.550
And I think that's true
for the NOF as well,

01:05:35.550 --> 01:05:36.930
which I know we'll talk about later.

01:05:36.930 --> 01:05:38.940
And then effectiveness, I'd say the same.

01:05:38.940 --> 01:05:40.080
So that's the first one.

01:05:40.080 --> 01:05:41.490
The second is you didn't talk

01:05:41.490 --> 01:05:44.910
about diabetes and the
things not going so well,

01:05:44.910 --> 01:05:46.050
and we've got this group,

01:05:46.050 --> 01:05:48.300
diabetes and cardiovascular disease,

01:05:48.300 --> 01:05:49.950
where, yeah, there's bit of improvement,

01:05:49.950 --> 01:05:51.480
but they're really...

01:05:51.480 --> 01:05:53.460
The diabetes one is going backwards.

01:05:53.460 --> 01:05:55.620
And I think considering, you know,

01:05:55.620 --> 01:05:59.463
that cardiovascular diabetes
remains our biggest killer,

01:06:00.450 --> 01:06:01.800
at least, you know, to be

01:06:01.800 --> 01:06:06.090
at such low absolute numbers
and deteriorating numbers,

01:06:06.090 --> 01:06:07.650
particularly for that diabetes care

01:06:07.650 --> 01:06:10.470
and for some aspects of
the cardiovascular disease,

01:06:10.470 --> 01:06:11.670
is not good.

01:06:11.670 --> 01:06:13.320
So what very...

01:06:13.320 --> 01:06:15.903
What's our big, focused
plan of work on that?

01:06:17.730 --> 01:06:19.440
- Well, just, if I bring
other colleagues in

01:06:19.440 --> 01:06:21.210
on diabetes and CVD,

01:06:21.210 --> 01:06:22.620
potentially Amanda, to answer that,

01:06:22.620 --> 01:06:25.200
but one thing we will discuss later

01:06:25.200 --> 01:06:26.250
is the opportunity, obviously,

01:06:26.250 --> 01:06:28.170
to deep-dive in a number of key areas.

01:06:28.170 --> 01:06:29.640
We've talked about maternity.

01:06:29.640 --> 01:06:32.400
I wonder whether CVD and
diabetes more generally

01:06:32.400 --> 01:06:34.650
might be a deep-dive area.

01:06:34.650 --> 01:06:35.483
Amanda.

01:06:35.483 --> 01:06:36.316
- Amanda, yeah.

01:06:36.316 --> 01:06:37.620
- Just on the diabetes,

01:06:37.620 --> 01:06:38.903
it's measured, as you know, through QOF,

01:06:38.903 --> 01:06:40.980
so it's an annual measure.

01:06:40.980 --> 01:06:44.460
For this current year so '25-'26,

01:06:44.460 --> 01:06:47.340
we shifted incentives in QOF

01:06:47.340 --> 01:06:49.140
to have a much, much greater focus

01:06:49.140 --> 01:06:50.820
on the things that contribute

01:06:50.820 --> 01:06:54.840
towards secondary prevention
of cardiovascular mortality,

01:06:54.840 --> 01:06:56.490
and that includes a greater focus

01:06:56.490 --> 01:06:59.340
on the diabetes and CVD indicators.

01:06:59.340 --> 01:07:00.660
We don't yet have the data

01:07:00.660 --> 01:07:02.760
to see whether that has been effective,

01:07:02.760 --> 01:07:05.220
but I would suggest that
what this measure does

01:07:05.220 --> 01:07:08.100
is looks at proportion
of people who had all

01:07:08.100 --> 01:07:11.300
of the interventions relating
to good diabetes care.

01:07:11.300 --> 01:07:12.990
We really need to break that down

01:07:12.990 --> 01:07:14.580
by individual interventions

01:07:14.580 --> 01:07:17.250
and look at where the
problems actually are,

01:07:17.250 --> 01:07:20.130
because I suspect it's not
just a general plan, yeah.

01:07:20.130 --> 01:07:21.810
- Okay, that's great, and
if we could also do that

01:07:21.810 --> 01:07:25.380
without exceptions, with
exceptions and without exceptions,

01:07:25.380 --> 01:07:26.430
that would be very helpful.

01:07:26.430 --> 01:07:28.740
- [Amanda] We do measure
exceptions across...

01:07:28.740 --> 01:07:30.810
- Yeah, so if we can see
the data, though, in this

01:07:30.810 --> 01:07:33.210
with and without, that would be...

01:07:33.210 --> 01:07:38.210
Okay, so I've got Matt and
Robert and Ravi, all right.

01:07:38.940 --> 01:07:40.050
I know you're...

01:07:40.050 --> 01:07:41.070
Robert.

01:07:41.070 --> 01:07:43.320
- Yeah, so under the "Improving Health

01:07:43.320 --> 01:07:45.840
and Reducing Inequality"
section, there's a...

01:07:45.840 --> 01:07:47.880
Slide eight is...

01:07:47.880 --> 01:07:48.893
It's shocking, I think,

01:07:48.893 --> 01:07:52.050
that in this day and age
we're seeing a decrease

01:07:52.050 --> 01:07:57.050
in healthy life expectancy, post-pandemic.

01:07:57.150 --> 01:07:59.250
So I mean, the comment is

01:07:59.250 --> 01:08:02.160
much of this is out with
the NHS, in a sense,

01:08:02.160 --> 01:08:03.930
but the emphasis in the 10-year plan

01:08:03.930 --> 01:08:06.090
to shift towards prevention

01:08:06.090 --> 01:08:09.630
just illustrates how
crucially important that is.

01:08:09.630 --> 01:08:12.600
The question is international comparisons

01:08:12.600 --> 01:08:14.763
because my understanding actually is

01:08:14.763 --> 01:08:18.150
that a number of EU
countries saw an improvement,

01:08:18.150 --> 01:08:19.680
arguably because some
of the most vulnerable

01:08:19.680 --> 01:08:21.900
were lost during COVID.

01:08:21.900 --> 01:08:25.773
But do you know how much
of an outlier we are?

01:08:29.910 --> 01:08:32.484
- Ravi was next and then Sam. Ravi, yep.

01:08:32.484 --> 01:08:34.700
- Just on four-hour performance
and 12-hour performance,

01:08:34.700 --> 01:08:36.360
do we really understand what is

01:08:36.360 --> 01:08:39.840
driving the slight monthly decline?

01:08:39.840 --> 01:08:44.280
And when do you think we
will get onto a trajectory

01:08:44.280 --> 01:08:47.613
where we'll actually going
to start meeting the target?

01:08:50.370 --> 01:08:53.670
And then second thing, just
on vaccines, I just wondered,

01:08:53.670 --> 01:08:56.460
in terms of the variation geographically

01:08:56.460 --> 01:08:58.020
on vaccine rates,

01:08:58.020 --> 01:09:01.950
is there anything we can learn
from the best performers?

01:09:01.950 --> 01:09:03.846
Is there much variation?

01:09:03.846 --> 01:09:04.713
- Okay, Sam.

01:09:05.970 --> 01:09:09.120
- Yeah, I think, David, mine
was on pickup of cancers

01:09:09.120 --> 01:09:10.567
and where we're picking them up:

01:09:10.567 --> 01:09:12.450
"Where are we heading in that direction?"

01:09:12.450 --> 01:09:15.390
So what's the sort of rate of positivity

01:09:15.390 --> 01:09:18.870
in the two-week referral
from primary care?

01:09:18.870 --> 01:09:23.040
And where are we in terms
of reducing the numbers

01:09:23.040 --> 01:09:27.270
that first present in A&amp;E with cancer?

01:09:27.270 --> 01:09:29.493
And then finally, what
are the sorts of things

01:09:29.493 --> 01:09:34.320
that we need to do; say, for
example, continuity of care;

01:09:34.320 --> 01:09:37.230
to up the early pickup.

01:09:37.230 --> 01:09:40.380
- Yep, okay, so if you maybe go vaccines,

01:09:40.380 --> 01:09:42.270
where's the variation,
what are we doing about it,

01:09:42.270 --> 01:09:43.590
how do we learn from it,

01:09:43.590 --> 01:09:45.300
how do we compare with other countries

01:09:45.300 --> 01:09:47.643
in terms of that overall life expectancy;

01:09:49.410 --> 01:09:52.560
I think Sam's point, I mean,
maybe Mark picks that up;

01:09:52.560 --> 01:09:54.570
Mark's been doing a lot of
work on all those questions;

01:09:54.570 --> 01:09:56.310
and then four-hour performance.

01:09:56.310 --> 01:10:00.690
- Yeah, so, firstly, in terms of vaccine,

01:10:00.690 --> 01:10:03.690
that's something we can
look into in greater detail.

01:10:03.690 --> 01:10:06.120
I don't know, Amanda, if you've got

01:10:06.120 --> 01:10:06.953
any further detail on that.

01:10:06.953 --> 01:10:10.080
- So, there is significant variation.

01:10:10.080 --> 01:10:11.880
With regard to the childhood vaccines,

01:10:11.880 --> 01:10:13.920
where we often see the biggest variations,

01:10:13.920 --> 01:10:15.450
there are specific parts of the country

01:10:15.450 --> 01:10:16.283
that are a challenge.

01:10:16.283 --> 01:10:19.953
London is always
significantly less served.

01:10:20.940 --> 01:10:23.370
They are, in fact, doing
quite a lot of work,

01:10:23.370 --> 01:10:24.510
and it's about...

01:10:24.510 --> 01:10:28.680
Its cultural and demographic issues

01:10:28.680 --> 01:10:29.850
that we're looking at.

01:10:29.850 --> 01:10:31.770
There's quite a lot of targeted work

01:10:31.770 --> 01:10:33.540
with specific communities.

01:10:33.540 --> 01:10:34.620
We're also looking

01:10:34.620 --> 01:10:37.380
at the general practise
contract next year,

01:10:37.380 --> 01:10:39.933
the way in which we
incentivize vaccine delivery.

01:10:40.980 --> 01:10:42.330
At its simplest, there's a bit

01:10:42.330 --> 01:10:45.090
of an all-or-nothing cliff edge.

01:10:45.090 --> 01:10:46.950
And what we want to do is incentivize

01:10:46.950 --> 01:10:49.170
really increasing the effort

01:10:49.170 --> 01:10:50.463
in those, in the most
challenged communities,

01:10:50.463 --> 01:10:52.950
so we're also looking at that,

01:10:52.950 --> 01:10:55.860
but we are conscious of that variation.

01:10:55.860 --> 01:10:58.815
- Think you're special advisor on .

01:10:58.815 --> 01:11:00.570
- Just a couple of additions on this.

01:11:00.570 --> 01:11:02.700
Actually, there's been
a huge amount of work

01:11:02.700 --> 01:11:04.020
through this summer,

01:11:04.020 --> 01:11:05.640
bringing all the best performers together

01:11:05.640 --> 01:11:06.810
with the worst performers.

01:11:06.810 --> 01:11:08.160
Where we've got challenges,

01:11:09.450 --> 01:11:12.123
we have brought those teams together.

01:11:13.230 --> 01:11:16.410
What is clear is where there's
a huge amount of creativity

01:11:16.410 --> 01:11:17.850
and where we're bringing the vaccine

01:11:17.850 --> 01:11:19.770
closer to where people are,

01:11:19.770 --> 01:11:23.100
there's a correlation
between that and uptake.

01:11:23.100 --> 01:11:24.450
It's not just about sitting back

01:11:24.450 --> 01:11:25.920
and having a smart campaign

01:11:25.920 --> 01:11:27.270
and expecting everybody to go to...

01:11:27.270 --> 01:11:30.990
You've got to take the
vaccine to where people are.

01:11:30.990 --> 01:11:32.400
It's also a leadership thing.

01:11:32.400 --> 01:11:35.370
So you know, throughout
the last few weeks,

01:11:35.370 --> 01:11:37.710
in every edition of "The Week,"

01:11:37.710 --> 01:11:39.510
we've been celebrating all of those folks

01:11:39.510 --> 01:11:42.690
that are getting the biggest
improvements this year.

01:11:42.690 --> 01:11:44.160
And I do think there's been a little bit

01:11:44.160 --> 01:11:46.650
of hiding behind vaccine hesitancy

01:11:46.650 --> 01:11:47.760
over the last couple of years

01:11:47.760 --> 01:11:49.710
in terms of putting a real effort in.

01:11:49.710 --> 01:11:52.290
There's some organisations
out there this year

01:11:52.290 --> 01:11:53.910
that've shown huge increases

01:11:53.910 --> 01:11:56.760
just by really putting some welly into it,

01:11:56.760 --> 01:11:57.930
so I think there's...

01:11:57.930 --> 01:11:58.980
There's been a lot of that.

01:11:58.980 --> 01:12:00.630
There's still clearly more to do.

01:12:01.500 --> 01:12:03.510
- I mean, just an anecdote,

01:12:03.510 --> 01:12:06.090
but from a local perspective on vaccine,

01:12:06.090 --> 01:12:07.620
particularly what's happening
right at the moment,

01:12:07.620 --> 01:12:10.920
we're seeing a lot less older people

01:12:10.920 --> 01:12:14.040
coming in with flu

01:12:14.040 --> 01:12:16.797
and seeing a much higher
demand in the younger people.

01:12:16.797 --> 01:12:19.020
And that is almost exclusively, we think,

01:12:19.020 --> 01:12:20.220
down to vaccination,

01:12:20.220 --> 01:12:21.990
which is a fantastic public health message

01:12:21.990 --> 01:12:24.030
which we need putting out.

01:12:24.030 --> 01:12:26.520
So, just in terms of life expectancy,

01:12:26.520 --> 01:12:27.353
the first thing we'll do

01:12:27.353 --> 01:12:28.920
is on that slide we will have a look

01:12:28.920 --> 01:12:32.640
at how we could get good
international comparative data

01:12:32.640 --> 01:12:33.510
that's relevant.

01:12:33.510 --> 01:12:35.280
I've heard the same as you

01:12:35.280 --> 01:12:36.450
with regards to, particularly, some

01:12:36.450 --> 01:12:39.060
of the Nordic countries,
again, outperforming.

01:12:39.060 --> 01:12:40.590
Dunno; I don't want to pick on you, Aidan;

01:12:40.590 --> 01:12:42.540
whether you had any idea
on international capacity.

01:12:42.540 --> 01:12:44.400
- I think the place to go

01:12:44.400 --> 01:12:48.690
is actually CMO reports recently
have had all these data in.

01:12:48.690 --> 01:12:50.340
And I don't have them to hand now,

01:12:50.340 --> 01:12:54.390
but they put us kind of
in the middle on this

01:12:54.390 --> 01:12:55.560
of Europe, and we shouldn't be.

01:12:55.560 --> 01:12:58.650
We should be better than
that, so it is of concern.

01:12:58.650 --> 01:13:01.170
And of course, it will
then inevitably lead

01:13:01.170 --> 01:13:04.050
to either greater demand, treatment costs,

01:13:04.050 --> 01:13:07.380
and impact on life
expectancy over time, so.

01:13:07.380 --> 01:13:08.910
- And quite a lot of
this is being picked up

01:13:08.910 --> 01:13:12.030
in the "Quality Strategy"
document, as well.

01:13:12.030 --> 01:13:13.020
We'll see, yep.

01:13:13.020 --> 01:13:15.251
- [David Probert] Four-hours
trajectory, Sarah-Jane.

01:13:15.251 --> 01:13:18.773
- Yeah, so, I think, as
colleagues have said,

01:13:18.773 --> 01:13:20.970
both of these metrics
are essentially stuck.

01:13:20.970 --> 01:13:23.220
They go up and down a
little bit based on demand,

01:13:23.220 --> 01:13:27.060
but there's not really been any
significant movement in them

01:13:27.060 --> 01:13:28.590
as there needs to be.

01:13:28.590 --> 01:13:30.840
So, we're currently doing a piece of work

01:13:30.840 --> 01:13:33.660
with the Royal College's
Emergency Medicine,

01:13:33.660 --> 01:13:35.670
Acute Medicine Society, and so on,

01:13:35.670 --> 01:13:40.257
to look at what a well-performing
emergency department

01:13:40.257 --> 01:13:42.060
and the services that sit behind

01:13:42.060 --> 01:13:43.890
so the Same Day Emergency Care,

01:13:43.890 --> 01:13:45.720
the medical assessment units,

01:13:45.720 --> 01:13:49.380
and to codify what the high performers,

01:13:49.380 --> 01:13:50.790
those that do achieve the target,

01:13:50.790 --> 01:13:52.590
because we do have a group that do,

01:13:52.590 --> 01:13:53.850
what is it that they do

01:13:53.850 --> 01:13:56.850
in terms of the clinical
and operational processes,

01:13:56.850 --> 01:13:59.700
how does it work, how
do the clinicians work.

01:13:59.700 --> 01:14:02.490
And then we'll have a big,
collective effort next year

01:14:02.490 --> 01:14:04.020
with leadership across the NHS

01:14:04.020 --> 01:14:08.010
to implement that in each
of our organisations.

01:14:08.010 --> 01:14:10.080
I don't think, if I'm
honest, at the moment,

01:14:10.080 --> 01:14:13.890
with the winter, you know, the
pressures that we're under...

01:14:13.890 --> 01:14:16.320
We need to focus on safety
rather than performance

01:14:16.320 --> 01:14:17.790
over the next six-to-eight weeks.

01:14:17.790 --> 01:14:19.740
But when we're out the other side,

01:14:19.740 --> 01:14:22.500
these best practise models
will be ready to go.

01:14:22.500 --> 01:14:24.300
And then we need to really work

01:14:24.300 --> 01:14:28.170
with the service and our
clinical and operational leaders

01:14:28.170 --> 01:14:30.970
to get them implemented through
the course of next year.

01:14:31.860 --> 01:14:34.890
- And then finally, just to
Sam's points around pickup,

01:14:34.890 --> 01:14:39.360
I will see how we could
include that data here.

01:14:39.360 --> 01:14:42.780
I can give an example from
a North London perspective

01:14:42.780 --> 01:14:44.610
across all of the providers there.

01:14:44.610 --> 01:14:46.560
The actual pickup of
cancer on the two-week

01:14:46.560 --> 01:14:48.000
hasn't changed dramatically

01:14:48.000 --> 01:14:50.850
in the key areas of prostate, breast.

01:14:50.850 --> 01:14:54.300
And GI improved as a result,
obviously, of bowel screening.

01:14:54.300 --> 01:14:56.700
The area where we have seen
significant improvement,

01:14:56.700 --> 01:15:00.680
and this also leads into
people presenting with cancer,

01:15:00.680 --> 01:15:03.060
is in lung and those associated

01:15:03.060 --> 01:15:05.070
with the screening programmes
that've been rolled out,

01:15:05.070 --> 01:15:06.990
where we're seeing that diagnostic change

01:15:06.990 --> 01:15:08.640
by almost a stage

01:15:08.640 --> 01:15:11.820
and much better interaction
with early presentation.

01:15:11.820 --> 01:15:14.400
But it's a really important
factor to your point

01:15:14.400 --> 01:15:16.140
around how effective two-week wait is,

01:15:16.140 --> 01:15:18.640
so we will look at how
best we could present that.

01:15:19.595 --> 01:15:21.208
- Mark, do you want to add anything?

01:15:21.208 --> 01:15:22.150
- The only thing I would add

01:15:22.150 --> 01:15:24.150
is I completely agree with David's point

01:15:24.150 --> 01:15:26.490
about the success with
the lung cancer screening

01:15:26.490 --> 01:15:28.350
because we're starting

01:15:28.350 --> 01:15:31.170
to pick up more Stage 1 and
2 cancers than previously,

01:15:31.170 --> 01:15:34.050
where we would see
patients presenting in ED

01:15:34.050 --> 01:15:36.057
with Stage 4 and 5

01:15:37.620 --> 01:15:40.380
and, obviously, with very
limited opportunities

01:15:40.380 --> 01:15:43.653
for interventions to have an
impact on a patient's life.

01:15:45.330 --> 01:15:48.090
We have seen an increase
in the number of referrals

01:15:48.090 --> 01:15:51.270
and patients coming through
the two-week wait pathway,

01:15:51.270 --> 01:15:52.800
but the incidence is not increasing

01:15:52.800 --> 01:15:54.660
beyond the modelling that we had assumed.

01:15:54.660 --> 01:15:55.530
So what we are seeing

01:15:55.530 --> 01:15:58.980
is, because there's long waits
in some elective pathways,

01:15:58.980 --> 01:16:01.200
increased use of urgent
suspected cancer route.

01:16:01.200 --> 01:16:03.000
So we're doing some work on this now

01:16:03.000 --> 01:16:06.420
to make sure that we have
the right referral options

01:16:06.420 --> 01:16:07.530
available to GPs

01:16:07.530 --> 01:16:09.563
to make sure patient
that are concerned about

01:16:09.563 --> 01:16:11.520
can get the appropriate treatment quickly

01:16:11.520 --> 01:16:15.540
but without sitting in the
queue on a cancer pathway.

01:16:15.540 --> 01:16:17.490
- But I think it's 5%, isn't it,

01:16:17.490 --> 01:16:18.323
at the moment?
- Is.

01:16:18.323 --> 01:16:19.156
- Yeah.

01:16:19.156 --> 01:16:21.630
- So 5% of referrals do have cancer.

01:16:21.630 --> 01:16:22.560
- [Mark Cubbon] Yeah.

01:16:22.560 --> 01:16:25.830
- Okay, right, shall we go on to finance?

01:16:25.830 --> 01:16:27.300
- Yep, happy to pick it up.

01:16:27.300 --> 01:16:29.288
So, month-six finance,

01:16:29.288 --> 01:16:32.690
so I'll pick it up in
three chunks, if I may.

01:16:32.690 --> 01:16:34.530
The first will be the revenue position.

01:16:34.530 --> 01:16:35.910
So, broadly balanced.

01:16:35.910 --> 01:16:39.270
Reporting a 55-million-pound overspend

01:16:39.270 --> 01:16:43.620
in non-ring-fenced RDEL
at the end of September.

01:16:43.620 --> 01:16:46.290
What you'll see there
within the table, table one,

01:16:46.290 --> 01:16:49.950
that the actual material

01:16:49.950 --> 01:16:51.810
or a large part of the overspend

01:16:51.810 --> 01:16:53.090
is actually at a system level,

01:16:53.090 --> 01:16:57.660
so that's 289 million at a service level.

01:16:57.660 --> 01:17:00.480
That's a combination of costs

01:17:00.480 --> 01:17:04.620
we believe related to industrial action.

01:17:04.620 --> 01:17:06.810
There's an element of
deficit support funding

01:17:06.810 --> 01:17:07.923
that's been held back,

01:17:08.760 --> 01:17:10.170
and we, obviously, will...

01:17:10.170 --> 01:17:12.570
We can see that some
of the provider plans,

01:17:12.570 --> 01:17:14.580
particularly around some
of the workforce changes

01:17:14.580 --> 01:17:17.340
that they anticipated
making earlier in the year,

01:17:17.340 --> 01:17:19.980
haven't actually materialised.

01:17:19.980 --> 01:17:22.680
Slippage on efficiency plans

01:17:22.680 --> 01:17:25.440
is one of our central drivers

01:17:25.440 --> 01:17:27.630
around some of the workforce costs.

01:17:27.630 --> 01:17:31.680
But again, we are looking at
this at quite a granular level,

01:17:31.680 --> 01:17:34.080
and it's not a universal picture.

01:17:34.080 --> 01:17:39.000
Previous years, we would've
seen it across all systems.

01:17:39.000 --> 01:17:41.370
It's worth highlighting,
perhaps, the system position.

01:17:41.370 --> 01:17:43.890
So, six systems account for more than half

01:17:43.890 --> 01:17:46.020
of the total overspend,

01:17:46.020 --> 01:17:49.290
and you can see that
information within the pack.

01:17:49.290 --> 01:17:51.780
And despite the position
that I'm describing,

01:17:51.780 --> 01:17:54.543
which is good, actually, relatively good,

01:17:55.770 --> 01:17:57.420
this is a much, much stronger position

01:17:57.420 --> 01:17:58.410
than we saw last year.

01:17:58.410 --> 01:18:00.930
So those six systems...

01:18:00.930 --> 01:18:01.980
Now we've got...

01:18:01.980 --> 01:18:06.150
So, it's 22 systems and 68% of providers

01:18:06.150 --> 01:18:07.770
are actually delivering their plan

01:18:07.770 --> 01:18:09.900
at this stage of the year, in month six.

01:18:09.900 --> 01:18:12.120
And that picture looks
like it's going through

01:18:12.120 --> 01:18:13.800
into month seven.

01:18:13.800 --> 01:18:14.970
This time last year,

01:18:14.970 --> 01:18:17.790
the variance would've been
around about 700 million.

01:18:17.790 --> 01:18:21.333
So, again, just in terms of relativity.

01:18:22.230 --> 01:18:23.490
The next topic, if I may,

01:18:23.490 --> 01:18:24.690
just because it's been a topic

01:18:24.690 --> 01:18:29.430
that some board members have
asked me about, is cash.

01:18:29.430 --> 01:18:31.380
I just want to assure everybody, actually,

01:18:31.380 --> 01:18:33.360
that at the start of the year we made sure

01:18:33.360 --> 01:18:36.960
that all providers, all
systems, had sufficient cash

01:18:36.960 --> 01:18:39.810
to actually deliver their
Board-approved plans.

01:18:39.810 --> 01:18:42.030
If there was anyone that
didn't have sufficient cash

01:18:42.030 --> 01:18:44.610
at the start of the year,
when we looked at their plans,

01:18:44.610 --> 01:18:48.120
we worked with them, and they
started with a cash injection.

01:18:48.120 --> 01:18:51.480
Clearly, that meant that
they had sufficient cash

01:18:51.480 --> 01:18:52.950
if they'd delivered their plan.

01:18:52.950 --> 01:18:57.030
If people go off plan, then,
obviously, people are starting

01:18:57.030 --> 01:19:00.120
to have some cash issues.

01:19:00.120 --> 01:19:02.160
The handful of providers
that have actually come in

01:19:02.160 --> 01:19:05.040
and requested PDC or cash support

01:19:05.040 --> 01:19:09.033
because of inadequate
spending control, et cetera,

01:19:09.870 --> 01:19:11.220
which is obviously
something that's very much

01:19:11.220 --> 01:19:12.690
a responsibility of the Board,

01:19:12.690 --> 01:19:15.150
we've worked very closely with those,

01:19:15.150 --> 01:19:17.520
absolutely ensuring that there is no risk

01:19:17.520 --> 01:19:19.110
to patients or staff,

01:19:19.110 --> 01:19:21.750
making sure they've got sufficient cash

01:19:21.750 --> 01:19:24.210
to continue to operate;

01:19:24.210 --> 01:19:27.330
however, for those particular
providers or systems

01:19:27.330 --> 01:19:30.360
that come in for cash support
because they've gone off plan,

01:19:30.360 --> 01:19:31.920
we put some strict conditions in.

01:19:31.920 --> 01:19:33.450
So we make sure they've got cash.

01:19:33.450 --> 01:19:34.560
We make sure there's no risk

01:19:34.560 --> 01:19:37.110
to staff, suppliers, or patients,

01:19:37.110 --> 01:19:40.980
but what we have got
is a level of scrutiny,

01:19:40.980 --> 01:19:44.370
and we're asking for clear
evidence at a board level

01:19:44.370 --> 01:19:47.940
that actions are being taken
to address the overspend.

01:19:47.940 --> 01:19:49.350
I think that's really important.

01:19:49.350 --> 01:19:51.210
And if I could just say the context;

01:19:51.210 --> 01:19:53.370
quite often, people talk
to me about the context

01:19:53.370 --> 01:19:56.160
of having to manage low cash levels

01:19:56.160 --> 01:19:59.010
and how difficult it can be;

01:19:59.010 --> 01:20:02.940
I think for every one person
that asks me about that,

01:20:02.940 --> 01:20:04.650
I've got more than two that are asking me

01:20:04.650 --> 01:20:08.043
about allocations at the moment
and the move to fair shares.

01:20:08.880 --> 01:20:10.290
So people are quite distressed

01:20:10.290 --> 01:20:14.610
that someone else is spending
their money and overspending,

01:20:14.610 --> 01:20:16.570
so effectively, we have to make sure

01:20:16.570 --> 01:20:19.587
we've got clear accountability
around cash management.

01:20:19.587 --> 01:20:21.210
But just to give Board assurance,

01:20:21.210 --> 01:20:24.300
you know, we are looking
at this very closely.

01:20:24.300 --> 01:20:28.260
The position on productivity
is within the pack.

01:20:28.260 --> 01:20:30.810
So, again, we're ahead of track,

01:20:30.810 --> 01:20:33.660
and an improving position month on month,

01:20:33.660 --> 01:20:36.060
which is always very positive.

01:20:36.060 --> 01:20:37.980
And then looking ahead to the year end,

01:20:37.980 --> 01:20:40.920
where we're, obviously,
forecasting we're on plan,

01:20:40.920 --> 01:20:43.440
there are some material back-loaded plans,

01:20:43.440 --> 01:20:46.590
so we are working closely
with 16 systems around that.

01:20:46.590 --> 01:20:49.560
But again, just to give
you, the Board, assurance,

01:20:49.560 --> 01:20:52.440
in the majority of places,
the run rate has improved

01:20:52.440 --> 01:20:55.080
and continues to improve at month seven.

01:20:55.080 --> 01:20:59.610
Our agency whole-time
equivalents are down 46%

01:20:59.610 --> 01:21:01.260
compared to last year.

01:21:01.260 --> 01:21:03.090
Our bank spend is down 10%,

01:21:03.090 --> 01:21:04.057
because a lot of people have said,

01:21:04.057 --> 01:21:06.960
"Have you just switched
from agency to bank?"

01:21:06.960 --> 01:21:09.930
And in total, the saving
across agency bank

01:21:09.930 --> 01:21:11.190
for this first part of the year

01:21:11.190 --> 01:21:16.190
is around about a billion
pounds more than last year.

01:21:16.350 --> 01:21:18.840
So all of the clear signs are telling us

01:21:18.840 --> 01:21:21.780
that this isn't people
manipulating numbers.

01:21:21.780 --> 01:21:24.150
We can see clear evidence year-to-date

01:21:24.150 --> 01:21:25.560
that these numbers are coming down,

01:21:25.560 --> 01:21:27.750
and I just want to say thank
you, actually, to everyone

01:21:27.750 --> 01:21:30.360
because it's been amazing work

01:21:30.360 --> 01:21:31.920
whilst at the same time we've heard

01:21:31.920 --> 01:21:34.260
about the performance improvements today.

01:21:34.260 --> 01:21:35.190
So really good,

01:21:35.190 --> 01:21:38.010
and I guess there are
some challenged systems,

01:21:38.010 --> 01:21:39.990
but through the mid-year review process,

01:21:39.990 --> 01:21:43.380
I don't think we came across one that said

01:21:43.380 --> 01:21:45.570
they weren't pushing ahead
for the remainder of this year

01:21:45.570 --> 01:21:47.820
to try to deliver their
plan and dig in deep.

01:21:48.960 --> 01:21:52.200
- Okay, good, thank you. Any other...

01:21:52.200 --> 01:21:53.033
Mark?

01:21:54.840 --> 01:21:57.630
- So, the big strategic
changes in the 10-year plan

01:21:57.630 --> 01:21:59.380
clearly haven't been delivered yet.

01:22:00.450 --> 01:22:04.144
So what changed from where
we were to where we are?

01:22:04.144 --> 01:22:06.480
Because this is a pretty
transformational change

01:22:06.480 --> 01:22:08.480
in the financial position of the system,

01:22:09.660 --> 01:22:11.880
down to, clearly, amazing
executive leadership.

01:22:11.880 --> 01:22:12.713
Apart from that.

01:22:16.408 --> 01:22:17.308
- I honestly think

01:22:19.680 --> 01:22:22.120
it's been the clarity and the transparency

01:22:24.218 --> 01:22:25.950
and the discipline, actually,

01:22:25.950 --> 01:22:29.730
of understanding there's no bailouts.

01:22:29.730 --> 01:22:31.500
There's no money for bailouts.

01:22:31.500 --> 01:22:33.183
You know, we've got...

01:22:33.183 --> 01:22:35.130
We worked really closely with systems

01:22:35.130 --> 01:22:36.330
during the planning stage

01:22:36.330 --> 01:22:38.760
to try and help them to get
to a place with robust plans

01:22:38.760 --> 01:22:39.780
at huge pace.

01:22:39.780 --> 01:22:41.520
So there are some aspects of the plans

01:22:41.520 --> 01:22:43.680
that like the activity
plan triangulation's

01:22:43.680 --> 01:22:45.390
not as good as it could be.

01:22:45.390 --> 01:22:46.770
But I honestly think,

01:22:46.770 --> 01:22:48.900
particularly speaking
to the CFO community,

01:22:48.900 --> 01:22:51.150
this is day in, day out

01:22:51.150 --> 01:22:55.860
and not just technical efficiency.

01:22:55.860 --> 01:22:58.740
This is about clinically led change

01:22:58.740 --> 01:22:59.910
that's happening on the ground,

01:22:59.910 --> 01:23:01.800
and there's pockets of it, actually.

01:23:01.800 --> 01:23:04.080
And when we come on to a
productivity conversation

01:23:04.080 --> 01:23:06.750
later today, we can describe to you

01:23:06.750 --> 01:23:09.300
where we can see just pockets
of excellence across the NHS,

01:23:09.300 --> 01:23:10.860
and we've just got to make sure

01:23:10.860 --> 01:23:14.520
we learn from that and
spread it across the piste.

01:23:14.520 --> 01:23:15.690
But just some of the statistics

01:23:15.690 --> 01:23:16.770
we'd talked about this morning,

01:23:16.770 --> 01:23:19.530
you know, half a billion
pounds that could be saved

01:23:19.530 --> 01:23:22.350
through not having some
A&amp;E attendances turn up

01:23:22.350 --> 01:23:24.000
that, quite frankly,
don't need to go there.

01:23:24.000 --> 01:23:26.070
They don't even need an alternative.

01:23:26.070 --> 01:23:27.030
They don't need anything.

01:23:27.030 --> 01:23:30.570
So there's a lot to go at,
and it feels quite exciting.

01:23:30.570 --> 01:23:34.260
But equally, I'm really conscious
of the operational context

01:23:34.260 --> 01:23:36.393
that Jim and Sarah-Jane's described.

01:23:37.800 --> 01:23:38.800
I was at the HFMA.

01:23:38.800 --> 01:23:41.370
There's a HFMA event going on currently,

01:23:41.370 --> 01:23:43.410
and I met with the young group

01:23:43.410 --> 01:23:45.060
of senior leaders coming through,

01:23:45.060 --> 01:23:46.590
and they said to me, oh, you know,

01:23:46.590 --> 01:23:48.667
your-job's-really-important type of thing:

01:23:48.667 --> 01:23:51.210
"What's the most difficult
thing about your job?"

01:23:51.210 --> 01:23:53.460
And I said, "Time."

01:23:53.460 --> 01:23:54.750
So much to go at.

01:23:54.750 --> 01:23:57.930
It's being able to make
sure that we are structured

01:23:57.930 --> 01:24:01.410
in a way that we can focus on
the things that matter most,

01:24:01.410 --> 01:24:02.970
because we can make a difference here.

01:24:02.970 --> 01:24:04.830
- We'll come back to that.

01:24:04.830 --> 01:24:08.760
HFMA is Healthcare Finance
Management Association.

01:24:08.760 --> 01:24:09.593
Is that right?

01:24:09.593 --> 01:24:10.426
- Yeah.
- It is, yeah.

01:24:10.426 --> 01:24:12.300
- Okay, so I've got Sam and then Glen.

01:24:12.300 --> 01:24:13.550
And then we will move on.

01:24:14.670 --> 01:24:16.050
- So I think I'm right in saying

01:24:16.050 --> 01:24:20.430
the figures around sickness
absence are four times the rate

01:24:20.430 --> 01:24:22.683
that you would expect
in the private sector.

01:24:24.030 --> 01:24:27.840
That creates massive clinical
and financial pressure.

01:24:27.840 --> 01:24:31.350
What sort of things have
you planned to address that?

01:24:31.350 --> 01:24:32.183
- Yeah, so-

01:24:32.183 --> 01:24:33.016
- Hang on, let's have

01:24:33.016 --> 01:24:34.830
Glen's question as well first.
- Sorry.

01:24:34.830 --> 01:24:36.690
- So, I was just going to talk

01:24:36.690 --> 01:24:38.910
about the work that
Elizabeth's team have done

01:24:38.910 --> 01:24:42.120
to get the planning framework
and the financial rules

01:24:42.120 --> 01:24:43.950
for next year into play,

01:24:43.950 --> 01:24:45.750
because I think the context

01:24:45.750 --> 01:24:49.350
of moving to a rules-based
fair-shares approach

01:24:49.350 --> 01:24:51.837
is affecting behaviour
already in this year.

01:24:51.837 --> 01:24:54.420
And I think that's part
of why it's improving.

01:24:54.420 --> 01:24:56.070
- Yeah, good. Helpful to hear that.

01:24:56.070 --> 01:24:57.884
So let's talk a bit about sickness.

01:24:57.884 --> 01:24:58.717
And then we'll-
- Yeah, I didn't know, Jo,

01:24:58.717 --> 01:25:00.510
if you wanted to come in as well.

01:25:00.510 --> 01:25:01.500
Do you want to go first, or?

01:25:01.500 --> 01:25:02.450
- Yep, thanks, Sam.

01:25:03.510 --> 01:25:05.310
Yeah, I mean, private sector count it

01:25:05.310 --> 01:25:06.630
differently to us, we would argue,

01:25:06.630 --> 01:25:07.897
but yes, it is over and
above what it should be.

01:25:07.897 --> 01:25:10.395
So in the 10-year plan, we said...

01:25:10.395 --> 01:25:12.030
It was about 5% now,

01:25:12.030 --> 01:25:13.530
but we set an ambition to reduce that

01:25:13.530 --> 01:25:16.950
by one percentage point to 4.1% per annum,

01:25:16.950 --> 01:25:21.328
which is the best it's been,
I think, since about 2014.

01:25:21.328 --> 01:25:23.094
So, that doesn't sound like a lot, 1%,

01:25:23.094 --> 01:25:26.900
but a 1% on 1.6 million
people is actually a lot.

01:25:26.900 --> 01:25:28.590
So there's a lot of work
we're putting into that

01:25:28.590 --> 01:25:30.990
as part of the medium-term planning guide.

01:25:30.990 --> 01:25:33.690
That's like one of the top
priorities the service have got,

01:25:33.690 --> 01:25:35.400
because bank and agency drive sickness,

01:25:35.400 --> 01:25:37.710
sorry, sickness rates drives
bank and agency rates,

01:25:37.710 --> 01:25:39.477
as you know.

01:25:39.477 --> 01:25:42.090
And we've just started to roll out

01:25:42.090 --> 01:25:46.050
a Staff Treatment Access service

01:25:46.050 --> 01:25:48.060
that provides help with mental health,

01:25:48.060 --> 01:25:50.670
musculoskeletal services,

01:25:50.670 --> 01:25:52.110
try and sort of give people the support

01:25:52.110 --> 01:25:53.400
that they need to reduce that.

01:25:53.400 --> 01:25:56.040
But it is going to be hard
yards, so, just to get it down,

01:25:56.040 --> 01:25:59.460
but that 1% is about
40,000 people every year

01:25:59.460 --> 01:26:02.790
and all the really close work
we're going to have to do

01:26:02.790 --> 01:26:04.350
with those staff in those places

01:26:04.350 --> 01:26:06.390
to improve that health.

01:26:06.390 --> 01:26:08.640
But we've got to do more in
the 10 Year Workforce Plan,

01:26:08.640 --> 01:26:10.290
what we can do as an employer

01:26:10.290 --> 01:26:12.840
to actually make our staff the healthiest

01:26:12.840 --> 01:26:14.880
compared to other workforces.

01:26:14.880 --> 01:26:17.100
I think there's a lot
more to go at, but we are.

01:26:17.100 --> 01:26:19.110
It is an absolute priority for us.

01:26:19.110 --> 01:26:20.640
- We'll come back and talk about that more

01:26:20.640 --> 01:26:21.900
into new year, okay.

01:26:21.900 --> 01:26:24.270
So thank you, Elizabeth.

01:26:24.270 --> 01:26:26.070
We've got one paper to note,

01:26:26.070 --> 01:26:27.150
which is about looking

01:26:27.150 --> 01:26:29.730
at improving equality,
diversity, and inclusion

01:26:29.730 --> 01:26:32.190
in health-and-care research.

01:26:32.190 --> 01:26:33.300
So that's helpful.

01:26:33.300 --> 01:26:34.623
Noted. Thank you.

01:26:35.970 --> 01:26:37.890
And then we've got four questions

01:26:37.890 --> 01:26:39.270
from members of the public,

01:26:39.270 --> 01:26:41.220
and I'm going to ask individual colleagues

01:26:41.220 --> 01:26:42.480
to respond to them.

01:26:42.480 --> 01:26:44.557
So first of all, Sarah-Jane,

01:26:44.557 --> 01:26:46.680
"can you share what's happening

01:26:46.680 --> 01:26:50.250
in terms of complex
paediatric transfer decisions

01:26:50.250 --> 01:26:52.230
and how we are ensuring that they're made

01:26:52.230 --> 01:26:55.314
lawfully and within
appropriate timeframes?"

01:26:55.314 --> 01:26:56.177
- Yeah, thanks, Chair.

01:26:56.177 --> 01:26:58.497
It's a really important question

01:26:58.497 --> 01:27:00.903
and one, with my background,
I feel very passionately about

01:27:00.903 --> 01:27:02.280
that we get right.

01:27:02.280 --> 01:27:04.410
So it's the duty of all clinical teams

01:27:04.410 --> 01:27:08.280
to record data about
decision-making around patients.

01:27:08.280 --> 01:27:12.000
Whether that's clinical teams
within the same organisation

01:27:12.000 --> 01:27:13.590
or, in the example of a transfer,

01:27:13.590 --> 01:27:17.160
the clinical teams
between the organisations,

01:27:17.160 --> 01:27:19.770
particularly where there might
be differences of opinion

01:27:19.770 --> 01:27:21.510
or anything of that nature,

01:27:21.510 --> 01:27:24.420
it's absolutely incumbent
for that to be documented.

01:27:24.420 --> 01:27:27.543
We do set out the standards
that we expect for this,

01:27:28.470 --> 01:27:30.840
particularly from our
specialist paediatric providers,

01:27:30.840 --> 01:27:33.180
where, often, the transfers are between,

01:27:33.180 --> 01:27:35.640
and they're in our national
service specifications,

01:27:35.640 --> 01:27:37.590
which are on our website.

01:27:37.590 --> 01:27:39.150
It's clear to all providers

01:27:39.150 --> 01:27:41.490
that they have to work
to those specifications.

01:27:41.490 --> 01:27:43.890
It's part of the standard contract.

01:27:43.890 --> 01:27:46.050
Where there's evidence of concern,

01:27:46.050 --> 01:27:47.670
then we'll always look at NHS England

01:27:47.670 --> 01:27:50.280
if there's any more than
we can do, but we think

01:27:50.280 --> 01:27:54.060
that the existing frameworks
and specifications that we have

01:27:54.060 --> 01:27:57.030
make it really clear what
the expectations are,

01:27:57.030 --> 01:27:58.530
and there's probably not more

01:27:58.530 --> 01:28:01.410
that mandating would
actually do to support this.

01:28:01.410 --> 01:28:03.330
But obviously, we'll constantly survey

01:28:03.330 --> 01:28:07.260
to see whether we've got any
issues that we need to address.

01:28:07.260 --> 01:28:08.093
- Thank you.

01:28:08.093 --> 01:28:10.680
And then question for Duncan, please.

01:28:10.680 --> 01:28:13.860
So, there have been quite a few questions

01:28:13.860 --> 01:28:15.390
about this Supreme Court ruling

01:28:15.390 --> 01:28:17.587
on the legal definition of a woman:

01:28:17.587 --> 01:28:20.520
"What immediate steps
will NHS England be taking

01:28:20.520 --> 01:28:23.820
to implement the Supreme
Court court ruling?"

01:28:23.820 --> 01:28:24.750
- Yeah, thanks Chair.

01:28:24.750 --> 01:28:27.120
So, following the Supreme Court ruling

01:28:27.120 --> 01:28:29.310
in April of this year
on the definition of sex

01:28:29.310 --> 01:28:32.310
in relation to the 2010 Equality Act,

01:28:32.310 --> 01:28:33.450
NHS England's been reviewing

01:28:33.450 --> 01:28:35.550
all relevant policies and procedures

01:28:35.550 --> 01:28:37.710
to ensure that we're
aligning with the ruling;

01:28:37.710 --> 01:28:39.300
however, it's really important

01:28:39.300 --> 01:28:43.950
that the Equality and Human
Rights Commission, so the EHRC,

01:28:43.950 --> 01:28:45.420
updated Code of Practise

01:28:45.420 --> 01:28:48.660
for services, public
functions, and associations,

01:28:48.660 --> 01:28:50.430
which sets out the legal framework

01:28:50.430 --> 01:28:51.960
for how services are delivered

01:28:51.960 --> 01:28:53.490
in line with the Equality Act,

01:28:53.490 --> 01:28:57.030
is available before any
final decisions can be taken

01:28:57.030 --> 01:28:58.290
about national policies.

01:28:58.290 --> 01:29:01.770
So the updated Code of Practise
will be published shortly,

01:29:01.770 --> 01:29:04.650
subject to parliamentary approval.

01:29:04.650 --> 01:29:05.970
And then we'll be able

01:29:05.970 --> 01:29:10.230
to update and issue
guidance following that.

01:29:10.230 --> 01:29:12.360
In terms of the questions
that've come through

01:29:12.360 --> 01:29:14.460
around data collection and recordings,

01:29:14.460 --> 01:29:16.230
the work's ongoing in a number of areas,

01:29:16.230 --> 01:29:18.630
such as the Unified Information Standard

01:29:18.630 --> 01:29:20.160
for Protected Characteristics

01:29:20.160 --> 01:29:23.460
to support the collection
of improved equality data

01:29:23.460 --> 01:29:25.110
on patients and employees

01:29:25.110 --> 01:29:26.730
around protected characteristics.

01:29:26.730 --> 01:29:28.620
This will enable NHS organisations

01:29:28.620 --> 01:29:30.930
to improve care and employment approaches

01:29:30.930 --> 01:29:34.020
and better respond to legal
duties around inequalities

01:29:34.020 --> 01:29:35.940
in relation to protected characteristics

01:29:35.940 --> 01:29:37.490
under the Equality Act of 2010.

01:29:39.210 --> 01:29:41.490
And then work is also
ongoing to address concerns

01:29:41.490 --> 01:29:44.550
related to clinical safety,
data integrity, and safeguarding

01:29:44.550 --> 01:29:46.110
that've arisen in relation to the creation

01:29:46.110 --> 01:29:48.330
of the new NHS numbers

01:29:48.330 --> 01:29:50.700
when patients change their gender

01:29:50.700 --> 01:29:53.640
and also the terminology
used on patient records

01:29:53.640 --> 01:29:55.830
in relation to sex and gender.

01:29:55.830 --> 01:29:57.390
This work intends to ensure

01:29:57.390 --> 01:29:59.880
that patients are not
unintentionally excluded

01:29:59.880 --> 01:30:02.580
from services related to biology,

01:30:02.580 --> 01:30:07.080
such as breast or cervical
cancer screening programmes.

01:30:07.080 --> 01:30:09.930
And then in terms of language

01:30:09.930 --> 01:30:12.480
used in health communications
at a national level,

01:30:12.480 --> 01:30:14.850
work has been ongoing
for a number of years now

01:30:14.850 --> 01:30:16.500
to ensure that information for patients

01:30:16.500 --> 01:30:18.600
on sex specific conditions and treatments

01:30:18.600 --> 01:30:20.520
is clearly understood.

01:30:20.520 --> 01:30:23.700
So, the approach taken on this
is to use additive language

01:30:23.700 --> 01:30:25.350
so, for example, explaining

01:30:25.350 --> 01:30:27.810
that cervical screening
services are available

01:30:27.810 --> 01:30:30.180
to women and people with a cervix

01:30:30.180 --> 01:30:33.750
to ensure that language
is clear and inclusive.

01:30:33.750 --> 01:30:36.390
So NHS organisations should always

01:30:36.390 --> 01:30:38.760
consult the NHS Data Model and Dictionary

01:30:38.760 --> 01:30:40.890
to ensure that local
information is aligned

01:30:40.890 --> 01:30:42.570
to best practise,

01:30:42.570 --> 01:30:44.070
so I'm sure we will update the Board

01:30:44.070 --> 01:30:47.550
as soon as we've made
changes to the guidance.

01:30:47.550 --> 01:30:49.560
- Okay, thank you, Duncan.

01:30:49.560 --> 01:30:51.247
And then for Elizabeth,

01:30:51.247 --> 01:30:55.170
"how does both NHS England and DHSC ensure

01:30:55.170 --> 01:30:57.300
that nationally agreed best prices

01:30:57.300 --> 01:30:59.910
secured to the NHS supply chain frameworks

01:30:59.910 --> 01:31:04.380
are implemented across ICBs and trusts?"

01:31:04.380 --> 01:31:08.543
- Okay, yeah so, we recommend
and encourage ICBs and trusts

01:31:08.543 --> 01:31:12.510
to use the NHS supply
chain to secure best value,

01:31:12.510 --> 01:31:14.640
but we don't actually mandate it

01:31:14.640 --> 01:31:18.060
outside of something we
call the NHS Core List,

01:31:18.060 --> 01:31:20.770
and therefore, we don't
formally report on this

01:31:22.020 --> 01:31:24.660
in terms of the use of the supply chain;

01:31:24.660 --> 01:31:27.420
however, there is quite
a lot of information

01:31:27.420 --> 01:31:31.950
that's available within the
NHS Spend Comparison Service,

01:31:31.950 --> 01:31:34.140
so that information is out there.

01:31:34.140 --> 01:31:36.840
We are working with the NHS supply chain

01:31:36.840 --> 01:31:41.840
to launch the NHS Core List
of products imminently,

01:31:42.090 --> 01:31:45.810
and as we've been talking
about the refresh on planning,

01:31:45.810 --> 01:31:48.360
that will actually go into
the standard contract.

01:31:48.360 --> 01:31:52.170
And that Core List goes
live in January, actually,

01:31:52.170 --> 01:31:53.910
so further progress on this,

01:31:53.910 --> 01:31:57.360
and phase two of the projects will go live

01:31:57.360 --> 01:31:59.760
in quarter one of '26-'27,

01:31:59.760 --> 01:32:01.740
so we'll start rolling that out.

01:32:01.740 --> 01:32:02.670
- Okay, thank you.

01:32:02.670 --> 01:32:04.417
And then final one, for Matthew,

01:32:04.417 --> 01:32:08.310
"why did NHS England allow
gender identity clinics

01:32:08.310 --> 01:32:10.980
to withhold outcome data
from the "Cass Review"?

01:32:10.980 --> 01:32:12.420
And what governance and oversight

01:32:12.420 --> 01:32:13.830
of gender identity clinics

01:32:13.830 --> 01:32:16.110
is currently in place?"

01:32:16.110 --> 01:32:17.400
- Thank you.

01:32:17.400 --> 01:32:18.480
So, it's worth being clear

01:32:18.480 --> 01:32:22.260
that Dr. Cass, in the
"Cass Review," recommended

01:32:22.260 --> 01:32:23.910
that it was necessary to complete

01:32:23.910 --> 01:32:26.285
both the data-linkage study

01:32:26.285 --> 01:32:28.727
and a wider programme of research.

01:32:28.727 --> 01:32:31.200
So NHS England, working

01:32:31.200 --> 01:32:33.000
with the Department of
Health and Social Care,

01:32:33.000 --> 01:32:37.200
are taking both of those
pieces of work forward.

01:32:37.200 --> 01:32:39.990
That includes work ensuring

01:32:39.990 --> 01:32:44.990
that the right statutory and
nonstatutory governance is

01:32:45.000 --> 01:32:47.250
in place for the data-linkage study.

01:32:47.250 --> 01:32:49.650
I'm working very closely with clinics

01:32:49.650 --> 01:32:53.280
to minimise any delays
in the sharing of data

01:32:53.280 --> 01:32:58.280
so that both of those pieces
of work can progress at pace.

01:32:58.770 --> 01:33:01.020
Alongside the data-linkage
study, as I said,

01:33:01.020 --> 01:33:03.120
the PATHWAYS Trial,

01:33:03.120 --> 01:33:04.740
it was launched by King's College London

01:33:04.740 --> 01:33:06.780
at the end of November,

01:33:06.780 --> 01:33:10.680
again, in line with Dr.
Cass's recommendations.

01:33:10.680 --> 01:33:12.573
In respect of the clinics,

01:33:13.440 --> 01:33:17.130
the transformation of those
gender services is progressing.

01:33:17.130 --> 01:33:22.130
We've already seen three
services go fully operational

01:33:22.560 --> 01:33:26.290
and work to the fundamentally
different model of care

01:33:27.330 --> 01:33:28.770
which has been set out.

01:33:28.770 --> 01:33:32.670
That's in the Northwest,
the Southwest, and London.

01:33:32.670 --> 01:33:34.950
There'll be a fourth service,
in the East of England,

01:33:34.950 --> 01:33:37.170
going live in the new year,

01:33:37.170 --> 01:33:40.140
and more will follow in '26-'27

01:33:40.140 --> 01:33:42.030
as part of the programme of work

01:33:42.030 --> 01:33:44.520
of improving both quality
and access of care

01:33:44.520 --> 01:33:46.023
for this population.

01:33:46.922 --> 01:33:48.227
- Okay, thank you.

01:33:48.227 --> 01:33:50.823
Okay, thank you all very much.

01:33:51.930 --> 01:33:54.560
That concludes the formal business.

01:33:54.560 --> 01:33:57.353
Is there anything anyone
else wants to raise?

01:33:58.390 --> 01:33:59.790
Nope, okay.

01:33:59.790 --> 01:34:03.720
So, I'd like to take this
opportunity, first of all,

01:34:03.720 --> 01:34:07.200
to thank people who
have turned up in person

01:34:07.200 --> 01:34:08.760
to join the Board

01:34:08.760 --> 01:34:12.150
or those who are watching
these proceedings.

01:34:12.150 --> 01:34:15.300
I'd very much like to thank
all of the staff out there.

01:34:15.300 --> 01:34:16.710
As Jim mentioned particularly,

01:34:16.710 --> 01:34:20.010
a number of people are
having to go above and beyond

01:34:20.010 --> 01:34:21.930
to cover for industrial action

01:34:21.930 --> 01:34:25.893
and cover across the holiday season.

01:34:26.970 --> 01:34:28.260
Particularly want to note...

01:34:28.260 --> 01:34:29.940
Elizabeth said, "What's the hardest thing?

01:34:29.940 --> 01:34:33.510
Time," and I think it
is probably fair to say

01:34:33.510 --> 01:34:36.300
that all of the executives
around this table, at the moment,

01:34:36.300 --> 01:34:38.430
are certainly working six-day weeks,

01:34:38.430 --> 01:34:41.850
if not seven-day weeks
for a number of people,

01:34:41.850 --> 01:34:44.040
and working very long days.

01:34:44.040 --> 01:34:45.363
And that is exhausting,

01:34:46.260 --> 01:34:48.600
so first off, thank you to everybody

01:34:48.600 --> 01:34:50.040
because people are absolutely going

01:34:50.040 --> 01:34:51.540
